/*
 * OCR Twenty First Century GCSE Chemistry B (J258) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real OCR Twenty First Century GCSE Chemistry B (J258)
 * questions are context-led: a student's investigation, a real-world
 * application, a table of results or a described graph opens each question,
 * practicals are examined in context, and maths skills run throughout. Every
 * item here follows that pattern, written at both Foundation-overlap and Higher
 * demand.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: one marking point per line with (1), allow / ignore
 * / do not accept notes, max n; 6-mark extended responses use the board's three-
 * level ladder with indicative content.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const CHEMISTRY_OCR_B_GCSE_PRACTICE = {
  'C1': {
    name: 'Air and Water',
    questions: [
      { q: `Scientists think that for the first billion years of the Earth's history there was intense volcanic activity. The volcanoes released gases that formed the Earth's early atmosphere.\n\nName the gas that scientists think made up most of the Earth's early atmosphere.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Carbon dioxide / CO₂ (1)\nIgnore: water vapour given alongside carbon dioxide\nDo not allow: oxygen / nitrogen` },
      { q: `Mia breathes out through a straw into a test tube of limewater to show that exhaled air contains carbon dioxide.\n\nWhat would Mia see if carbon dioxide is present?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• Limewater turns milky / cloudy (1)\nAllow: white precipitate forms\nIgnore: bubbles` },
      { q: `A carbon monoxide alarm is fitted next to a gas boiler in a house.\n\nWhy is carbon monoxide dangerous to people?\n\nTick (✓) one box.\n\n☐ It causes acid rain.\n☐ It is a greenhouse gas that warms the house.\n☐ It reduces the amount of oxygen the blood can carry.\n☐ It makes the air cloudy with soot.`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• It reduces the amount of oxygen the blood can carry (1)\nIf more than one box is ticked, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `The Earth's atmosphere today is mostly nitrogen and oxygen, with small amounts of other gases.\n\nWhat is the approximate percentage of nitrogen in dry air today?\n\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\n\n0.04 %        1 %        21 %        78 %`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• 78 % (1)\nIf more than one answer is ringed, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Water from a reservoir passes through several stages at a water treatment works before it is piped to homes.\n\nName the stage of water treatment that kills harmful microorganisms in the water.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Chlorination / adding chlorine (1)\nAllow: ozone treatment / UV treatment\nIgnore: filtration / sedimentation` },
      { q: `On a cold morning, droplets of water form on the inside of a car windscreen. Water vapour in the air inside the car has turned into liquid water.\n\nName this change of state.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Condensing / condensation (1)\nDo not allow: evaporation / freezing` },
      { q: `Zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid. A gas is given off.\n\nzinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen\n\nDescribe the test for hydrogen gas and give the result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Hold a lighted / burning splint at the mouth of the test tube (1)\n• (Squeaky) pop (1)\nDo not allow: glowing splint for the first mark\nThe second mark is dependent on a splint being used.` },
      { q: `Ben adds a small amount of manganese dioxide to some hydrogen peroxide solution in a boiling tube. Bubbles of oxygen gas are given off quickly.\n\nDescribe how Ben could test the gas to show that it is oxygen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Put a glowing splint into the tube / the gas (1)\n• The splint relights (1)\nDo not allow: lighted / burning splint for the first mark\nIgnore: "burns brighter" for the second mark unless a glowing splint has been used` },
      { q: `Chlorine gas is very toxic. A leak of chlorine gas from a storage tank is suspected at a swimming pool plant room. An engineer uses a piece of indicator paper to check the air near the tank.\n\nDescribe the test the engineer should use for chlorine and give the positive result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (Hold) damp / moist (blue) litmus paper in the gas (1)\n• Turns red and then is bleached / turns white (1)\nAllow: damp universal indicator paper is bleached\nAllow: damp litmus paper is bleached (for 2 marks)` },
      { q: `The table shows the approximate composition of dry air today.\n\nGas | Percentage by volume (%)\nnitrogen | 78\noxygen | 21\nargon | 0.9\ncarbon dioxide | 0.04\n\nA gas syringe contains 250 cm³ of dry air.\n\nCalculate the volume of oxygen in the syringe.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 21 ÷ 100 × 250 / 0.21 × 250 (1)\n• = 52.5 cm³ (1)\nAllow: 52 or 53 cm³\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `About 2.7 billion years ago, simple organisms called cyanobacteria evolved in the oceans. Rocks formed before this time contain very little iron oxide. Rocks formed after this time contain layers of iron oxide.\n\nExplain how the evolution of these organisms changed the composition of the Earth's atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The organisms photosynthesised / took in carbon dioxide (1)\n• and released oxygen, so the percentage of oxygen increased (and carbon dioxide decreased) (1)\nIgnore: references to iron oxide alone` },
      { q: `The Earth's early atmosphere contained a much higher percentage of carbon dioxide than the atmosphere today.\n\nDescribe two ways in which carbon dioxide was removed from the early atmosphere, other than by photosynthesis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans (1)\n• It was locked up in carbonate rocks / limestone / sedimentary rocks (from shells and skeletons of sea creatures) (1)\n• It was locked up in fossil fuels (coal, oil, natural gas) (1)\nIgnore: "absorbed by plants" (photosynthesis excluded by the question)` },
      { q: `Ali heats a sample of solid wax in a boiling tube in a water bath. He records the temperature every 30 seconds. The temperature of the wax stays at 54 °C for 3 minutes while the wax melts, even though energy is still being transferred to it.\n\nExplain why the temperature stays the same while the wax is melting.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The energy (transferred) is used to overcome / weaken the forces (of attraction) between the particles (1)\n• so the (kinetic) energy of the particles / temperature does not increase (until all the wax has melted) (1)\nDo not allow: energy is used to break bonds within the particles / molecules / covalent bonds` },
      { q: `Air can be cooled until it becomes a liquid. The table shows the boiling points of three gases in air.\n\nGas | Boiling point (°C)\nargon | −186\nnitrogen | −196\noxygen | −183\n\nA sample of liquid air is slowly warmed from −200 °C.\n\nWhich gas will boil first? Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Nitrogen (1)\n• It has the lowest boiling point / it reaches its boiling point first (as the temperature rises) (1)\nThe second mark is dependent on nitrogen being chosen.` },
      { q: `In the particle model, the particles of a substance are drawn as small, hard spheres. A textbook diagram shows the particles of a gas as 10 circles spread out in a box.\n\nGive two limitations of this model for representing the particles in a real gas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Real particles are not solid / hard / inelastic spheres (1)\n• The particles are not to scale / the spaces between gas particles are much larger than shown (1)\n• It does not show the forces (of attraction) between particles (1)\n• It does not show the movement / speed of the particles (1)\n• A real gas contains far more particles (1)\n• Real particles may be molecules with different shapes, not single spheres (1)` },
      { q: `Natural gas is mainly methane, CH₄. It is burned in gas boilers to heat homes. When there is plenty of air, complete combustion takes place.\n\nWrite a balanced symbol equation for the complete combustion of methane.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O (2)\nCorrect formulae of reactants and products, not balanced (1)\nAllow: multiples, e.g. 2CH₄ + 4O₂ → 2CO₂ + 4H₂O\nIgnore: state symbols` },
      { q: `A gas boiler has a blocked air vent. The flame in the boiler turns yellow and black soot forms inside the boiler.\n\nExplain why the boiler now produces soot and carbon monoxide.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Not enough oxygen / air (reaches the fuel), so incomplete combustion takes place (1)\n• Some carbon (in the fuel) is only partly oxidised to carbon monoxide, and some is not oxidised at all and is released as carbon particles / soot (1)` },
      { q: `Coal burned in some power stations contains small amounts of sulfur as an impurity.\n\nExplain how burning this coal can damage limestone buildings many kilometres from the power station.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Sulfur reacts with oxygen to make sulfur dioxide (when the coal burns) (1)\n• Sulfur dioxide dissolves in rain water / reacts with water to form acid rain, which reacts with / damages limestone (1)\nAllow: SO₂ for sulfur dioxide` },
      { q: `Petrol does not contain nitrogen compounds. However, the exhaust gases from a petrol car engine contain nitrogen monoxide, NO.\n\nExplain why nitrogen monoxide is formed in a car engine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Nitrogen and oxygen from the air (react) (1)\n• because of the high temperature in the engine (1)\nDo not allow: nitrogen comes from the fuel` },
      { q: `Diesel engines release tiny solid particles of carbon called particulates. Some cities have high levels of particulates in the air on busy roads.\n\nDescribe two problems caused by particulates in the air.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• They cause breathing / respiratory problems / damage lungs / make asthma worse (1)\n• They can cause heart disease / lung cancer (1)\n• They make buildings dirty / blacken buildings (1)\n• They cause global dimming / reflect sunlight / reduce sunlight reaching the ground (1)\nIgnore: "pollution" unqualified` },
      { q: `A catalytic converter in a car exhaust changes harmful gases into less harmful gases. In one reaction, carbon monoxide reacts with nitrogen monoxide to make carbon dioxide and nitrogen.\n\nWrite a balanced symbol equation for this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂ (2)\nCorrect formulae, not balanced, e.g. CO + NO → CO₂ + N₂ (1)\nAllow: CO + NO → CO₂ + ½N₂ (2)` },
      { q: `A researcher collects data from five towns (data written for this practice question).\n\nTown | Mean NO₂ in air (µg/m³) | Asthma hospital visits per 10 000 people per year\nA | 18 | 21\nB | 25 | 26\nC | 31 | 33\nD | 40 | 38\nE | 47 | 45\n\nThe researcher says, "These data prove that nitrogen dioxide causes asthma attacks."\n\nExplain why the data do not prove this.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The data only show a (positive) correlation / that both increase together (1)\n• Another factor could be the cause, e.g. other pollutants such as particulates, traffic levels, smoking, age or income of the population (1)\nAllow: only five towns / small sample, so the pattern may be due to chance (for second mark)\nAllow: no mechanism is shown (for second mark)` },
      { q: `Some gases in the atmosphere are greenhouse gases.\n\nWhich two gases are greenhouse gases?\n\nTick (✓) two boxes.\n\n☐ argon\n☐ carbon dioxide\n☐ hydrogen\n☐ methane\n☐ nitrogen\n☐ oxygen`, marks: 2, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide (1)\n• methane (1)\nFor each extra box ticked beyond two, deduct 1 mark.` },
      { q: `Without any greenhouse gases in the atmosphere, the average temperature of the Earth's surface would be about −18 °C. The actual average temperature is about 15 °C.\n\nExplain how greenhouse gases keep the Earth's surface warmer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The Earth's surface emits / radiates infrared radiation (after being warmed by the Sun) (1)\n• Greenhouse gases absorb (and re-emit) some of this infrared radiation, so energy is kept in the atmosphere / less energy escapes to space (1)\nDo not allow: greenhouse gases "trap the Sun's rays" / reflect radiation back for the second mark\nDo not allow: references to the ozone layer` },
      { q: `Air bubbles trapped in Antarctic ice cores show that the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere in 1750 was about 280 parts per million (ppm). Direct measurements show that in 2023 it was about 420 ppm.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in carbon dioxide concentration between 1750 and 2023.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (420 − 280) ÷ 280 × 100 / 140 ÷ 280 × 100 (1)\n• = 50 % (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `Scientists use computer climate models to predict the rise in global average temperature by the year 2100. Different models give predictions ranging from about 1.5 °C to over 4 °C.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the predictions from different climate models are different.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Future emissions of greenhouse gases are not known / depend on what people and governments do (1)\n• Models make different assumptions / include different factors (e.g. clouds, oceans, ice melting) (1)\n• The climate is a very complex system / not all processes are fully understood (1)\n• Different starting data are used / data have uncertainty (1)\n• Feedback effects (e.g. melting ice, release of methane) are hard to predict (1)` },
      { q: `Water from a river is treated to make it potable. The table shows three stages of treatment.\n\nStage: sedimentation, filtration, chlorination\nPurpose (in a different order):\n• kills microorganisms\n• lets large insoluble particles settle to the bottom\n• removes small insoluble particles by passing the water through sand\n\nDraw lines to connect each stage with its correct purpose. Write your answer as three pairs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', markScheme: `• sedimentation — lets large insoluble particles settle to the bottom\n• filtration — removes small insoluble particles by passing the water through sand\n• chlorination — kills microorganisms\nAll three correct (2)\nOne or two correct (1)\nIf more than one line is drawn from a stage, that stage is wrong.` },
      { q: `Chlorine is added to drinking water at a concentration of about 0.5 mg per litre. Chlorine is toxic at high concentrations. Some people say that chlorine should not be added to drinking water.\n\nExplain why water companies decide that the benefit of adding chlorine is greater than the risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Chlorine kills microorganisms that cause (serious / deadly) diseases such as cholera / typhoid (1)\n• The concentration used is very low so the risk of harm is very small (compared with the risk of disease) (1)` },
      { q: `A student mixes two pairs of solutions in polystyrene cups and measures the temperature before and after mixing.\n\nMixture | Start temperature (°C) | End temperature (°C)\nP: citric acid + sodium hydrogencarbonate | 20 | 15\nQ: hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide | 20 | 27\n\nWhich mixture shows an endothermic reaction? Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• P (1)\n• The temperature decreases / falls (by 5 °C), so energy is taken in from the surroundings (1)\nThe second mark is dependent on P being chosen.` },
      { q: `Methane burns in oxygen in an exothermic reaction. A spark is needed to start the reaction in a gas cooker.\n\nDraw a reaction profile for the combustion of methane. Label the reactants, the products and the activation energy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• Products drawn at a lower energy level than reactants, with a curve (hump) rising above the reactants between them (1)\n• Activation energy shown by an arrow from the reactant level up to the top of the hump, and reactants and products labelled (1)\nIgnore: labelling of the overall energy change` },
      { q: `On a cold night, water vapour in the air condenses on a window to form droplets of liquid water.\n\nExplain the changes in the movement and arrangement of the particles when water vapour condenses. Use ideas about energy in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Particles lose (kinetic) energy / slow down (as they transfer energy to the cold window) (1)\n• Particles move from being far apart / randomly spread out to being close together / touching (but still random) (1)\n• Forces of attraction between particles become strong enough to hold them together, so they can only move past / slide over each other (1)` },
      { q: `Methane burns in oxygen:\n\nCH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O\n\nThe table shows some bond energies.\n\nBond | Bond energy (kJ/mol)\nC–H | 413\nO=O | 498\nC=O | 805\nO–H | 464\n\nCalculate the energy change for this reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Energy to break bonds = (4 × 413) + (2 × 498) = 2648 kJ/mol (1)\n• Energy released making bonds = (2 × 805) + (4 × 464) = 3466 kJ/mol (1)\n• Energy change = 2648 − 3466 = −818 kJ/mol (1)\nAllow: 818 kJ/mol released / exothermic for the third mark\nAllow ECF from incorrect totals` },
      { q: `Scientists study rocks to find out how the atmosphere has changed. Banded iron formations are rocks containing layers of iron oxide. They formed in the oceans between about 2.5 and 1.8 billion years ago. Iron oxide forms when dissolved iron reacts with oxygen.\n\nA scientist writes: "The iron oxide layers show that oxygen was being released into the oceans by photosynthesising organisms."\n\nWhich part of the scientist's statement is data and which part is an explanation? Explain why scientists cannot be certain that the explanation is correct.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Data: (the presence of) iron oxide layers in the rocks (1)\n• Explanation: oxygen was released by photosynthesising organisms (1)\n• The explanation cannot be observed directly / the events happened billions of years ago / oxygen could have come from another source (so the explanation is inferred) (1)` },
      { q: `Some coal-fired power stations use flue gas desulfurisation. The waste gases are passed through a wet mixture of calcium oxide (lime) before they leave the chimney.\n\nExplain how this reduces pollution, and why it is needed for coal but not for most natural gas.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Sulfur dioxide (in the waste gases) reacts with the calcium oxide / is removed from the gases (1)\n• so less sulfur dioxide is released, which reduces acid rain (1)\n• Coal contains sulfur impurities but (most) natural gas has had the sulfur removed / contains little sulfur (1)\nAllow: calcium sulfite / calcium sulfate / gypsum is formed (for the first mark)` },
      { q: `A city introduced a low emission zone in 2019. Older, more polluting vehicles must pay a charge to drive in the zone. The table shows the annual mean concentration of nitrogen dioxide at one roadside monitoring site (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Annual mean NO₂ (µg/m³)\n2018 | 52\n2023 | 38\n\nThe city council says: "The low emission zone has reduced air pollution in the city."\n\nEvaluate the council's statement. Use the data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• Supports: NO₂ fell by 14 µg/m³ / by about 27 % (1)\n• Against: data from only one site / only two years / NO₂ is only one pollutant, so may not represent the whole city (1)\n• Against: another factor could explain the fall, e.g. newer cleaner cars nationally, less traffic (e.g. in the pandemic), weather (1)\nAllow: no repeat measurements / no comparison with a city without a zone` },
      { q: `Carbon capture and storage (CCS) is one way of reducing the amount of carbon dioxide released into the atmosphere. Carbon dioxide from a power station is removed from the waste gases and pumped into rocks deep underground.\n\nExplain why CCS on its own is not enough to deal with climate change, and give one other way of reducing carbon dioxide emissions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Problem of scale: it can only be used at large single sources / power stations, not cars and homes / the amount stored is small compared with total emissions (1)\n• Risk / cost: it is expensive / uses energy / carbon dioxide could leak from storage (1)\n• One other way: use renewable energy (wind, solar) / nuclear power / improve energy efficiency / insulate homes / use electric vehicles / plant trees (1)` },
      { q: `Sam wants to make a sample of pure water from salty water in the laboratory.\n\nDescribe how Sam could do this, and how Sam could show that the water collected is pure.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Heat the salty water (in a flask) so the water boils / evaporates and the salt is left behind (1)\n• Cool the water vapour in a condenser and collect the liquid water (1)\n• Pure water boils at exactly 100 °C / freezes at exactly 0 °C (1)\nAllow: no residue left when a sample of collected water is evaporated (for the third mark)` },
      { q: `Kai heats a solid called stearic acid until it melts. He then lets it cool and records its temperature every minute (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime (min) | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7\nTemperature (°C) | 80 | 74 | 69 | 69 | 69 | 69 | 63 | 58\n\nUse the data to give the melting point of stearic acid, and explain what is happening to the particles between 2 and 5 minutes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Melting point = 69 °C (1)\n• Between 2 and 5 minutes the stearic acid is freezing / changing from liquid to solid (1)\n• Forces of attraction form between the particles, which releases energy, so the temperature stays constant / the particles settle into fixed positions in a regular arrangement (1)` },
      { q: `Leah uses damp iron wool to find the percentage of oxygen in air. She puts the iron wool in a tube connected to a gas syringe containing 100 cm³ of air. The iron rusts and uses up the oxygen. After a week she reads the volume of gas left in the syringe.\n\nLeah repeats the experiment four times (data written for this practice question).\n\nExperiment | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nVolume of gas left (cm³) | 79 | 80 | 78 | 81\n\nCalculate the mean percentage of oxygen in air from Leah's results, and give the uncertainty in her result. Suggest one reason why her mean is lower than the accepted value of 21 %.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Volumes of oxygen used: 21, 20, 22, 19 cm³ (1)\n• Mean = 82 ÷ 4 = 20.5 % (1)\n• Uncertainty: ± 1.5 % (range 19 % to 22 %) (1)\n• Reason: not all the oxygen had reacted / reaction not complete / air leaked into the apparatus / temperature was different when the final reading was taken (1)\nAllow: mean of 79.5 cm³ left, so 20.5 % (for the first two marks)` },
      { q: `A coastal town in a dry country needs a new supply of potable water. The water company considers three possible sources (data written for this practice question).\n\nSource | Treatment needed | Energy needed (kWh per m³ of water) | Daily volume available (m³)\nGround water from an aquifer | filtration, chlorination | 0.3 | 2 000\nWaste water from homes (sewage) | screening, sedimentation, aeration with bacteria, filtration, chlorination | 1.0 | 8 000\nSea water | distillation | 12 | unlimited\n\nThe town needs 10 000 m³ of water per day.\n\nEvaluate the three sources of water for the town. Use the information in the table.\n\nIn your answer you should:\n• explain why each source needs the treatment shown\n• compare the advantages and disadvantages of each source\n• recommend how the town should obtain its water.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains why each source needs its treatment AND compares the sources using the data AND makes a justified recommendation that meets the 10 000 m³ demand.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains the treatment for at least two sources AND makes some comparison using the data, OR makes a recommendation with limited justification.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Describes some treatment stages OR makes a simple comparison of the sources.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nTreatment\n• Ground water is already filtered through rock so it is fairly clean; filtration removes remaining insoluble solids; chlorination kills microorganisms\n• Waste water contains large solids, organic matter and many harmful microorganisms; screening removes large objects; sedimentation lets solids settle; aeration supplies oxygen so bacteria break down organic matter; then filtration and chlorination\n• Sea water contains dissolved salts that cannot be removed by filtration; distillation boils the water and condenses the vapour, leaving salts behind\nComparison\n• Ground water uses least energy (0.3 kWh/m³) so is cheapest, but only 2 000 m³ per day — not enough on its own; aquifer may run dry in a dry country\n• Waste water: 8 000 m³ per day; more stages and more energy (1.0 kWh/m³) than ground water; people may not accept drinking recycled sewage\n• Sea water: unlimited supply but needs 40 times as much energy as ground water (12 ÷ 0.3), so very expensive; burning fossil fuels for energy releases carbon dioxide (unless renewable energy, e.g. solar, is used); brine waste\nRecommendation\n• Ground water (2 000) + waste water (8 000) = 10 000 m³ meets demand with low energy use\n• Distillation of sea water could be used as a back-up in droughts or if the aquifer is depleted\n• Any justified recommendation that uses the data` }
    ]
  },
  'C2': {
    name: 'Chemical Patterns',
    questions: [
      { q: `Our model of the atom has changed over time as scientists found new evidence.\nIn 1803 John Dalton suggested a model of the atom.\n\nWhich two statements describe Dalton's model of the atom?\nTick (✓) two boxes.\nAtoms are tiny solid spheres that cannot be divided.\nAtoms have a small, positively charged nucleus.\nAll the atoms of one element are identical.\nElectrons orbit the nucleus in fixed shells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• Atoms are tiny solid spheres that cannot be divided. (1)\n• All the atoms of one element are identical. (1)\nIf more than two boxes are ticked, deduct one mark for each incorrect tick.` },
      { q: `In 1897 J.J. Thomson carried out experiments which showed that all atoms contain tiny, negatively charged particles. He called them electrons.\nThomson used this evidence to suggest the 'plum pudding' model of the atom.\n\nDescribe the plum pudding model of the atom.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• a sphere / ball of positive charge (1)\n• with (negative) electrons embedded / scattered / dotted through it (1)\nALLOW 'positive pudding with negative plums' for 1 mark only if both ideas are clear.\nIGNORE references to a nucleus (this is not part of Thomson's model).` },
      { q: `Geiger and Marsden fired positively charged alpha particles at a very thin sheet of gold foil. They recorded where the alpha particles went.\nThese are their observations:\n• Most of the alpha particles went straight through the foil.\n• A few alpha particles were deflected through large angles.\n• A very small number of alpha particles (about 1 in 8000) bounced back towards the source.\n\nExplain what these observations told Rutherford about the structure of the atom.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• most of the atom is empty space (because most alpha particles went straight through) (1)\n• the centre / nucleus is positively charged (because it repelled / deflected the positive alpha particles) (1)\n• the nucleus is very small (compared to the atom) and contains most of the mass (because very few bounced back) (1)\nALLOW 'mass concentrated in the centre' for 'most of the mass in the nucleus'.\nDO NOT ALLOW 'electrons deflected the alpha particles'.` },
      { q: `Rutherford's model of the atom had a small, positive nucleus with electrons moving around it.\nIn 1913 Niels Bohr changed Rutherford's model so that it agreed with new experimental observations.\n\nDescribe how Bohr's model of the atom is different from Rutherford's model.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• electrons are in shells / orbits / energy levels (1)\n• at fixed / particular distances from the nucleus / each shell has a fixed energy (1)\nIGNORE references to neutrons.` },
      { q: `Rutherford's model of the atom could not explain why most atoms have a mass about twice the mass of the protons they contain.\nIn 1932 James Chadwick found evidence for another particle in the nucleus.\n\nName the particle that Chadwick discovered.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `neutron (1)` },
      { q: `Atoms contain three types of particle: protons, neutrons and electrons.\nA student makes a table about the three particles. Some of the information is missing.\n\nParticle | Relative charge | Relative mass | Where found in the atom\nproton | +1 | 1 | ………………\nneutron | ……………… | 1 | nucleus\nelectron | ……………… | ……………… | in shells around the nucleus\n\nComplete the table.`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• proton – where found: nucleus (1)\n• neutron – relative charge: 0 (1)\n• electron – relative charge: −1 (1)\n• electron – relative mass: very small (1)\nALLOW 'no charge' / 'neutral' for 0.\nALLOW 1/1840, 1/2000, 0.0005 or 'almost zero' for very small.\nALLOW 0 for the relative mass of an electron only if written as ≈ 0 or "about 0".` },
      { q: `In Rutherford's experiment the gold foil was very thin.\nThe diameter of a gold atom is about 2.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\nA piece of gold foil is 8.6 × 10⁻⁷ m thick (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate approximately how many gold atoms thick the foil is.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (number of atoms =) 8.6 × 10⁻⁷ ÷ 2.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ (1)\n• = 2965.5… (1)\n• = 3000 / 3.0 × 10³ (to 2 significant figures) (1)\nALLOW 3000 or 3.0 × 10³ with no working for 3 marks.\nALLOW 2966 / 2970 for 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Chlorine is used to kill microorganisms in swimming pools.\nNatural chlorine contains two types of chlorine atom:\n³⁵₁₇Cl and ³⁷₁₇Cl\n\nExplain why these two atoms are isotopes of chlorine.\nUse the numbers of particles in each atom in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• both have the same number of protons / 17 protons (so both are chlorine) (1)\n• different numbers of neutrons / chlorine-35 has 18 neutrons and chlorine-37 has 20 neutrons (1)\nALLOW 'same atomic number, different mass number' for 1 mark only.\nIGNORE electrons.` },
      { q: `Calcium ions are found in hard water.\nThe Periodic Table box for calcium shows: atomic number 20, symbol Ca, relative atomic mass 40.1.\nAn atom of calcium-40 loses two electrons to form a Ca²⁺ ion.\n\nGive the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in a calcium-40 ion, Ca²⁺.\nNumber of protons = ……\nNumber of neutrons = ……\nNumber of electrons = ……`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `protons = 20, neutrons = 20, electrons = 18\nAll three correct (2)\nTwo correct (1)` },
      { q: `Aluminium is used to make drinks cans. Its atomic number is 13.\n\nWrite the electron arrangement of an aluminium atom.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2.8.3 (1)\nALLOW 2,8,3 or 2:8:3.` },
      { q: `Magnesium is used in emergency flares because it burns with a very bright white light.\nThe atomic number of magnesium is 12.\n\nDraw a diagram to show the electron arrangement in an atom of magnesium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• three shells drawn around a central nucleus (1)\n• with 2, 8 and 2 electrons from the inside outwards (1)\nALLOW dots or crosses for electrons.\nALLOW 2.8.2 written beside a correct diagram, but the diagram must show the electrons in shells.` },
      { q: `In 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev published a Periodic Table. About 60 elements were known at that time.\n\nDescribe two ways that Mendeleev arranged the elements in his Periodic Table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• in order of (increasing) atomic mass / atomic weight (1)\n• elements with similar (chemical) properties in the same group / column (1)\n• he left gaps for elements not yet discovered (1)\n• he swapped the order of some elements to fit their properties (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'in order of atomic number / proton number'.` },
      { q: `Mendeleev left a gap below silicon in his Periodic Table. He predicted the properties of the missing element, which he called eka-silicon.\nGermanium was discovered in 1886.\n\nProperty | Mendeleev's prediction for eka-silicon | Germanium\nrelative atomic mass | 72 | 72.6\ndensity of element in g/cm³ | 5.5 | 5.32\nformula of oxide | EsO₂ | GeO₂\ndensity of oxide in g/cm³ | 4.7 | 4.23\n\nExplain how the data in the table helped scientists to accept Mendeleev's Periodic Table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the properties of germanium are (very) close to / match Mendeleev's predictions (1)\n• so his predictions were tested and confirmed / this showed the patterns in his table were correct (so the table was accepted) (1)\nALLOW a correct comparison of any data pair from the table (e.g. 72 predicted, 72.6 measured) for the first mark.` },
      { q: `In his Periodic Table, Mendeleev mostly placed elements in order of atomic mass.\nHe placed tellurium (relative atomic mass 127.6) before iodine (relative atomic mass 126.9).\nIn the modern Periodic Table, tellurium has atomic number 52 and iodine has atomic number 53.\n\nExplain why Mendeleev placed tellurium before iodine, and why this order is correct in the modern Periodic Table.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• iodine has similar properties to chlorine and bromine / Group 7 elements, so it needed to go in that group (1)\n• so he ignored / did not follow the order of atomic mass (1)\n• the modern table is in order of atomic number / proton number and tellurium has a smaller atomic number (52) than iodine (53) (1)\nALLOW 'tellurium has similar properties to sulfur / selenium' for the first mark.` },
      { q: `A student burns a piece of magnesium ribbon and a piece of sulfur in oxygen.\nShe shakes each oxide with water and adds universal indicator.\n\nOxide | Colour of universal indicator | pH\nmagnesium oxide | purple | 10\nsulfur dioxide | red | 3\n\nWhat do these results suggest about the oxides of metals and the oxides of non-metals?`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• metal oxides (dissolve to) form alkaline solutions / are basic (1)\n• non-metal oxides (dissolve to) form acidic solutions / are acidic (1)\nALLOW 'magnesium oxide is alkaline, sulfur dioxide is acidic' for 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `A teacher adds a small piece of sodium to a large trough of water. The water contains a few drops of universal indicator.\nThe teacher stands behind a safety screen.\n\nDescribe three things the students see during the reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• sodium floats (1)\n• fizzing / bubbles / effervescence (1)\n• sodium moves (quickly) around on the surface (1)\n• sodium melts into a (silvery) ball (1)\n• sodium gets smaller / disappears (1)\n• universal indicator turns blue / purple (1)\nIGNORE 'hydrogen is given off' (not an observation).\nIGNORE 'it explodes' / 'burns with a lilac flame'.` },
      { q: `Lithium reacts with water to make an alkaline solution and a gas.\nlithium + water → lithium hydroxide + hydrogen\n\nWrite a balanced symbol equation for this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2Li + 2H₂O → 2LiOH + H₂\n• correct formulae: Li, H₂O, LiOH and H₂ (1)\n• correctly balanced (1)\nALLOW multiples.\nALLOW correct state symbols but IGNORE incorrect ones.\nDO NOT ALLOW the balancing mark if any formula is wrong.` },
      { q: `The table shows the melting points of some Group 1 metals.\n\nMetal | Melting point in °C\nlithium | 181\nsodium | 98\npotassium | 63\nrubidium | 39\ncaesium | ?\n\nDescribe the trend in melting point down Group 1, and predict the melting point of caesium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• melting point decreases down the group (1)\n• caesium predicted value in the range 20–35 °C (1)\n(The actual value is 28 °C.)\nALLOW 'gets lower' for decreases.` },
      { q: `Potassium reacts more vigorously with water than sodium does.\nSodium has the electron arrangement 2.8.1 and potassium has the electron arrangement 2.8.8.1.\n\nExplain why potassium is more reactive than sodium.\nUse ideas about electrons in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• potassium's outer electron is further from the nucleus / in a shell further out (1)\n• (so there is) less / weaker attraction between the nucleus and the outer electron / more shielding by inner shells (1)\n• so the outer electron is lost more easily (1)\nALLOW 'potassium has more shells' for the first mark.\nDO NOT ALLOW 'potassium has more electrons in its outer shell'.` },
      { q: `The halogens are in Group 7 of the Periodic Table.\nThe table shows some information about three halogens at room temperature.\n\nHalogen | State at room temperature | Colour\nchlorine | gas | green\nbromine | ……………… | red-brown\niodine | solid | ………………\n\nComplete the table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• bromine state: liquid (1)\n• iodine colour: grey / black / grey-black / dark purple-black (1)\nIGNORE 'shiny'.\nDO NOT ALLOW 'purple' alone (this is the colour of the vapour).` },
      { q: `A student adds bromine water to sodium iodide solution. The colourless solution turns brown.\nIodine and sodium bromide are made.\n\nWrite the word equation and the balanced symbol equation for this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• bromine + sodium iodide → sodium bromide + iodine (1)\n• Br₂ + 2NaI → 2NaBr + I₂ (1)\nALLOW multiples in the symbol equation.\nALLOW correct state symbols, e.g. (aq).` },
      { q: `When chlorine water is added to potassium bromide solution, the solution turns orange.\nThe balanced symbol equation is:\nCl₂(aq) + 2KBr(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + Br₂(aq)\n\nWrite the ionic equation for this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂\n• correct species, with no K⁺ / spectator ions (1)\n• correctly balanced including charges (1)\nALLOW state symbols, e.g. Cl₂(aq) + 2Br⁻(aq) → 2Cl⁻(aq) + Br₂(aq).\nALLOW K⁺ shown on both sides for 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Fluorine reacts explosively with hydrogen, even in the dark.\nIodine only reacts with hydrogen when it is heated.\nFluorine is at the top of Group 7 and iodine is lower down the group.\n\nExplain why the reactivity of the halogens decreases down Group 7.\nUse ideas about electrons in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• halogen atoms react by gaining an electron (to complete the outer shell) (1)\n• down the group the outer shell is further from the nucleus / there are more shells (1)\n• so there is weaker attraction for the extra electron / an electron is gained less easily (1)\nALLOW 'more shielding' for weaker attraction.\nDO NOT ALLOW answers in terms of losing electrons for the first or third marks.` },
      { q: `Argon is used to fill some light bulbs. It does not react with the hot metal filament.\nThe electron arrangement of argon is 2.8.8.\n\nExplain why argon is unreactive.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (argon has a) full / stable outer shell (of electrons) (1)\n• so it does not need to / does not easily lose, gain or share electrons (1)\nALLOW '8 electrons in the outer shell' for a full outer shell.\nIGNORE 'it is a noble gas'.` },
      { q: `The noble gases are in Group 0 of the Periodic Table.\nThe table shows the boiling points of five noble gases.\n\nNoble gas | Boiling point in °C\nhelium | −269\nneon | −246\nargon | −186\nkrypton | −153\nxenon | −108\nradon | ?\n\nDescribe the trend in boiling point down Group 0, and predict the boiling point of radon.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `• boiling point increases down the group (1)\n• radon predicted in the range −50 °C to −80 °C (1)\n(The actual value is −62 °C.)\nDO NOT ALLOW a positive value for the prediction.` },
      { q: `Party balloons are often filled with helium. In the past, some balloons and airships were filled with hydrogen.\nBoth gases are less dense than air.\n\nGive one reason why helium is used rather than hydrogen.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `helium is unreactive / does not burn / is not flammable (so it is safer) (1)\nALLOW 'hydrogen is flammable / explosive'.\nIGNORE 'helium is lighter'.` },
      { q: `Sodium reacts with chlorine to make sodium chloride.\nThe electron arrangement of sodium is 2.8.1 and the electron arrangement of chlorine is 2.8.7.\n\nExplain how sodium atoms and chlorine atoms form ions when they react.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a sodium atom loses one electron to form Na⁺ / a positive ion (1)\n• a chlorine atom gains one electron to form Cl⁻ / a negative ion (1)\n• the electron is transferred from sodium to chlorine and both ions have full outer shells / Na⁺ is 2.8 and Cl⁻ is 2.8.8 (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'shares electrons' for the third mark.` },
      { q: `Magnesium oxide is used to line furnaces.\nThe electron arrangement of magnesium is 2.8.2 and the electron arrangement of oxygen is 2.6.\n\nDraw a dot and cross diagram to show the ions in magnesium oxide.\nShow the outer shell electrons only.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• magnesium ion shown with an empty outer shell or with 8 electrons in its (new) outer shell (1)\n• oxide ion shown with 8 outer electrons, 6 of one symbol and 2 of the other (1)\n• charges shown as 2+ on magnesium and 2− on oxygen (1)\nALLOW Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ in brackets.\nALLOW the third mark if charges are correct even if the electron arrangements are not.` },
      { q: `Sodium chloride is an ionic compound. The table shows some of its properties.\n\nProperty | Sodium chloride\nmelting point | 801 °C\nconducts electricity when solid? | no\nconducts electricity when molten? | yes\n\nExplain why sodium chloride has a high melting point.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (giant lattice with) strong (electrostatic) attraction / forces between oppositely charged ions (1)\n• lots of energy is needed to overcome these forces / to separate the ions (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'strong covalent bonds' or 'intermolecular forces' for the first mark.\nDO NOT ALLOW 'breaking molecules'.` },
      { q: `Sodium chloride does not conduct electricity when it is solid. It does conduct electricity when it is molten or dissolved in water.\n\nExplain this difference.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• in the solid the ions are fixed in place / cannot move (1)\n• when molten or dissolved the ions are free to move (and carry the charge) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'electrons move' for either mark.` },
      { q: `The table shows the formulae of some ions.\n\nPositive ions: K⁺, Ca²⁺, Al³⁺\nNegative ions: Cl⁻, O²⁻\n\nWrite the formula of aluminium oxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Al₂O₃ (1)\nIGNORE charges if shown correctly, e.g. (Al³⁺)₂(O²⁻)₃.` },
      { q: `Some ionic compounds are used as food additives and in medicines.\nThe table shows the formulae of some ions.\n\nPositive ions: K⁺, Ca²⁺\nNegative ions: Cl⁻, O²⁻\n\nWrite the formulae of:\ncalcium chloride ……………\npotassium oxide ……………`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• CaCl₂ (1)\n• K₂O (1)\nIGNORE charges if shown correctly, e.g. Ca²⁺(Cl⁻)₂.\nDO NOT ALLOW CaCl or KO.` },
      { q: `A teacher uses a ball-and-stick model to show the structure of solid sodium chloride. Grey balls represent sodium ions and green balls represent chloride ions. All the balls are the same size and are joined by sticks.\n\nGive two limitations of this model.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the ions are not to scale / chloride ions are larger than sodium ions (1)\n• the ions are not joined by sticks / the sticks suggest bonds are physical links or covalent bonds (1)\n• the ions are (much) closer together / the model has too much space between ions (1)\n• it only shows a small part of the (giant) lattice (1)\n• it does not show the ions vibrating / the charges on the ions (1)\nIGNORE references to colour.` },
      { q: `Magnesium oxide and sodium chloride are both ionic compounds with giant lattice structures.\nMagnesium oxide contains Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ ions. It melts at 2852 °C.\nSodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions. It melts at 801 °C.\n\nExplain why magnesium oxide has a much higher melting point than sodium chloride.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the ions in magnesium oxide have larger charges / 2+ and 2− compared to 1+ and 1− (1)\n• so the (electrostatic) attraction between ions is stronger and more energy is needed to overcome it (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW references to covalent bonds or intermolecular forces.` },
      { q: `Magnesium chloride is used to melt ice on roads.\nThe formula of magnesium chloride is MgCl₂.\nRelative atomic masses: Mg = 24.3, Cl = 35.5\n\nCalculate the relative formula mass of magnesium chloride.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `95.3 (1)\n(24.3 + 2 × 35.5 = 95.3)` },
      { q: `Calcium hydroxide is used by farmers to neutralise acidic soil.\nThe formula of calcium hydroxide is Ca(OH)₂.\nRelative atomic masses: Ca = 40.1, O = 16.0, H = 1.0\n\nCalculate the relative formula mass of calcium hydroxide.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 40.1 + 2 × (16.0 + 1.0) / 40.1 + 32.0 + 2.0 (1)\n• = 74.1 (1)\nALLOW 74.1 with no working for 2 marks.\nALLOW 58.1 (bracket ignored, 40.1 + 16.0 + 2.0) for 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Potassium is put into a gas jar containing bromine vapour. A white solid, potassium bromide, KBr, is made.\nAt room temperature, potassium is a solid and bromine is a liquid.\npotassium + bromine → potassium bromide\n\nWrite a balanced symbol equation, with state symbols, for the reaction between potassium and liquid bromine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2K(s) + Br₂(l) → 2KBr(s)\n• correct formulae and balancing (1)\n• correct state symbols (1)\nALLOW Br₂(g) if the student refers to the vapour.\nALLOW multiples.` },
      { q: `A student adds chlorine water to potassium iodide solution. The equation for the reaction is:\nCl₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + I₂(aq)\n\nWhat does the state symbol (aq) mean?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `dissolved in water / aqueous solution (1)\nIGNORE 'liquid' alone.` },
      { q: `Iron is a transition metal and sodium is a Group 1 metal.\nThe table shows some information about the two metals.\n\nProperty | Iron | Sodium\nmelting point in °C | 1538 | 98\ndensity in g/cm³ | 7.87 | 0.97\ncolour of its sulfate compound | pale green | white\n\nUse the table to describe two differences between the properties of transition metals and Group 1 metals.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• transition metals have higher melting points (1)\n• transition metals have higher densities / are denser (1)\n• transition metals form coloured compounds (Group 1 compounds are white / colourless) (1)\nALLOW comparisons stated the other way round (e.g. 'Group 1 metals have lower melting points').\nALLOW correct comparisons using the values for iron and sodium.` },
      { q: `A student investigates the reactivity of the halogens.\nShe adds a few drops of each halogen solution to solutions of potassium chloride, potassium bromide and potassium iodide. The potassium halide solutions are all colourless.\nThe table shows her results.\n\nHalogen solution added | potassium chloride | potassium bromide | potassium iodide\nchlorine water | — | turns orange | turns brown\nbromine water | no change | — | turns brown\niodine solution | no change | no change | —\n\nExplain what the results show about the reactivity of chlorine, bromine and iodine.\nIn your answer you should:\n• use the results to put the halogens in order of reactivity\n• include equations for the reactions that take place\n• explain the trend in terms of electron arrangement.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nUses the results to give the correct order of reactivity with justification AND gives correct equations for at least two reactions AND explains the trend using ideas about electrons and distance from the nucleus.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nGives the correct order of reactivity with some use of the results AND EITHER gives at least one correct equation OR gives a partial explanation in terms of electrons.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nGives the order of reactivity OR identifies that a more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one OR gives one correct word equation.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nOrder and use of results\n• order of reactivity: chlorine > bromine > iodine\n• chlorine displaces bromine (orange) and iodine (brown), so it is the most reactive\n• bromine displaces iodine but not chlorine\n• iodine displaces neither, so it is the least reactive\n• a more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from its compound\nEquations\n• chlorine + potassium bromide → potassium chloride + bromine\n• Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂\n• Cl₂ + 2KI → 2KCl + I₂\n• Br₂ + 2KI → 2KBr + I₂\n• ionic, e.g. Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ / Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂ / Br₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Br⁻ + I₂\nExplanation\n• halogen atoms have 7 outer electrons and react by gaining one electron\n• down the group the atoms have more shells / the outer shell is further from the nucleus\n• more shielding / weaker attraction of the nucleus for the extra electron\n• so an electron is gained less easily and reactivity decreases down the group` }
    ]
  },
  'C3': {
    name: 'Chemicals of the Natural Environment',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the reactivity of metals.\n\nA student adds small pieces of four metals to dilute hydrochloric acid. The table shows the student's observations.\n\nMetal | Observation\ncopper | no bubbles\niron | a few bubbles form slowly\nmagnesium | lots of bubbles form quickly\nzinc | bubbles form steadily\n\nWhich metal is the most reactive?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `• magnesium (1)\nIgnore: Mg` },
      { q: `Copper is extracted from copper oxide by heating it with carbon.\n\ncopper oxide + carbon → copper + carbon dioxide\n\nPut a ring around the word that describes what happens to the copper oxide in this reaction.\n\ncombusted        neutralised        oxidised        reduced`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• reduced (1)\nIf more than one word is ringed, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Crude oil is separated into fractions by fractional distillation. Four of the fractions are listed below.\n\nTick (✓) one box to show the fraction that has the lowest boiling point.\n\n☐ bitumen\n☐ diesel\n☐ petrol\n☐ refinery gases`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• refinery gases only ticked (1)\nIf more than one box is ticked, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `The alkanes are a homologous series of hydrocarbons. The general formula of the alkanes is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\n\nHexadecane is an alkane found in the diesel fraction of crude oil. A molecule of hexadecane contains 16 carbon atoms.\n\nPredict the molecular formula of hexadecane.`, marks: 1, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `• C₁₆H₃₄ (1)\nAllow: C16H34` },
      { q: `A student dissolves some ammonium nitrate in water in a polystyrene cup. The temperature of the water falls from 20.5 °C to 14.0 °C.\n\nIs dissolving ammonium nitrate an exothermic or an endothermic change?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• endothermic (1)\nIgnore: reasons` },
      { q: `A teacher electrolyses concentrated sodium chloride solution, NaCl(aq), using inert electrodes. Bubbles of gas form at both electrodes.\n\nName the gas produced at the negative electrode (cathode).`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• hydrogen (1)\nAllow: H₂\nDo not allow: H / sodium` },
      { q: `A student puts an iron nail into a test tube of blue copper sulfate solution and leaves it for one hour.\n\nThe word equation for the reaction is:\niron + copper sulfate → iron sulfate + copper\n\nDescribe two things the student would see during the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• pink / brown / orange-brown solid / coating forms on the nail (1)\n• blue colour of the solution fades / becomes paler (1)\n• solution turns green / pale green (1)\nIgnore: nail dissolves / gets smaller\nIgnore: copper forms (not an observation)` },
      { q: `Railway lines can be welded together using the thermite reaction. A mixture of aluminium powder and iron oxide is ignited. Molten iron is made.\n\naluminium + iron oxide → aluminium oxide + iron\n\nExplain why aluminium can remove the oxygen from iron oxide.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• aluminium is more reactive than iron (1)\n• so aluminium has a greater tendency to combine with oxygen / aluminium displaces iron (from its oxide) (1)\nAllow: aluminium is higher in the reactivity series than iron` },
      { q: `Haematite is an ore of iron. It contains iron oxide, Fe₂O₃.\n\nRelative atomic masses: Fe = 56, O = 16\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of iron in iron oxide, Fe₂O₃.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mr of Fe₂O₃ = (2 × 56) + (3 × 16) = 160 (1)\n• % iron = 112 ÷ 160 × 100 = 70 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer of 70 (%) with no working scores 2 marks.\nIf 56 ÷ 160 × 100 = 35 (%), award 1 mark.` },
      { q: `A mining company finds a new deposit of copper ore. Tests show that the ore contains 0.8% copper by mass.\n\nCalculate the mass of ore, in tonnes, that must be processed to produce 2.0 tonnes of copper.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 2.0 ÷ 0.8 × 100 / 2.0 ÷ 0.008 (1)\n• = 250 (tonnes) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks.\nIf 2.0 × 0.008 = 0.016 or 1.6, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Zinc is extracted by heating zinc oxide with carbon in a furnace.\n\nZnO + C → Zn + CO\n\nWhich substance is oxidised and which substance is reduced in this reaction?\n\nGive a reason for each answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• carbon is oxidised because it gains oxygen (1)\n• zinc oxide is reduced because it loses oxygen (1)\nBoth substance and reason needed for each mark.\nAllow: zinc is reduced for zinc oxide is reduced` },
      { q: `A piece of zinc is placed in copper sulfate solution. The ionic equation for the reaction is:\n\nZn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s)\n\nExplain, in terms of electrons, which species is oxidised and which is reduced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• zinc (atoms) is oxidised because it loses (two) electrons (1)\n• copper ions / Cu²⁺ are reduced because they gain (two) electrons (1)\nAllow: OILRIG used correctly\nIf species identified correctly but no reference to electrons, max 1 mark.` },
      { q: `The list shows part of the reactivity series. The most reactive metal is at the top.\n\npotassium\nsodium\naluminium\n(carbon)\nzinc\niron\ncopper\n\nExplain why aluminium is extracted from aluminium oxide by electrolysis and not by heating with carbon.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• aluminium is more reactive than carbon / aluminium is above carbon in the reactivity series (1)\n• so carbon cannot reduce aluminium oxide / cannot remove the oxygen from aluminium oxide / cannot displace aluminium (1)\nIgnore: electrolysis is cheaper` },
      { q: `In industry, aluminium is extracted by the electrolysis of aluminium oxide. Aluminium oxide has a melting point of about 2050 °C. Before electrolysis it is dissolved in molten cryolite at about 950 °C.\n\nExplain why the aluminium oxide is dissolved in molten cryolite.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ions must be free to move for electrolysis / so it conducts electricity (1)\n• dissolving in cryolite means a lower temperature is needed (than melting aluminium oxide) so less energy is used / reduces (energy) costs (1)\nAllow: lowers the melting point (of the electrolyte)` },
      { q: `A teacher electrolyses molten lead bromide, PbBr₂, in a fume cupboard using carbon electrodes.\n\nComplete the table to show the product at each electrode.\n\nElectrode | Product\nnegative electrode (cathode) | ……………\npositive electrode (anode) | ……………`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• cathode: lead (1)\n• anode: bromine (1)\nDo not allow: bromide for bromine\nIf products reversed, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Molten lead bromide, PbBr₂, contains Pb²⁺ ions and Br⁻ ions. It is electrolysed using inert electrodes.\n\nWrite a balanced half equation for the reaction at each electrode.\n\nAt the cathode: ……………\nAt the anode: ……………`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• cathode: Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb (1)\n• anode: 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻ (1)\nAllow: 2Br⁻ − 2e⁻ → Br₂\nAllow: multiples\nIgnore: state symbols` },
      { q: `Sodium can be made by electrolysing molten sodium chloride. When a solution of sodium chloride in water is electrolysed instead, sodium is not produced at the cathode.\n\nExplain why sodium is not produced at the cathode when sodium chloride solution is electrolysed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the solution also contains hydrogen ions (from water) (1)\n• sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so hydrogen (gas) is produced instead (1)\nAllow: hydrogen ions are discharged / gain electrons more easily than sodium ions` },
      { q: `A student electrolyses copper sulfate solution, CuSO₄(aq), using inert carbon electrodes.\n\nPredict the product at each electrode.\n\nNegative electrode (cathode): ……………\nPositive electrode (anode): ……………`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `• cathode: copper (1)\n• anode: oxygen (1)\nDo not allow: sulfur / sulfate / hydrogen` },
      { q: `A student tests whether solid potassium iodide conducts electricity. It does not. The student then melts some potassium iodide and tests it again. Molten potassium iodide conducts electricity.\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• in the solid, the ions are held in fixed positions / cannot move (1)\n• when molten, the ions are free to move (and carry charge) (1)\nDo not allow: electrons move (0 for that marking point)` },
      { q: `Many old copper mines have waste heaps containing low-grade ore. One way of extracting the copper is called bioleaching.\n\nDescribe how bioleaching is used to obtain copper from low-grade ore.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• bacteria are used / added to the ore (1)\n• (bacteria) produce a solution containing copper ions / copper compounds (leachate), from which copper is then obtained (by displacement with scrap iron or electrolysis) (1)` },
      { q: `Phytoextraction uses plants to extract metals such as copper from contaminated soil. The plants are grown, harvested and burned. The ash contains copper compounds.\n\nGive one advantage and one disadvantage of using phytoextraction rather than mining and smelting copper ore.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Advantage, any one from:\n• can use low-grade ore / contaminated land / waste heaps (1)\n• uses less energy / produces less carbon dioxide / less damage to landscape than mining (1)\n• cleans up contaminated land (1)\nDisadvantage, any one from:\n• slow / takes a long time (months or years) for plants to grow (1)\n• low yield / only small amounts of copper produced (1)\n• needs a large area of land (1)\n• weather dependent (1)` },
      { q: `A company makes drinks cans from aluminium. A Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) is carried out for cans made from newly extracted aluminium and for cans made from recycled aluminium.\n\nGive two reasons why the LCAs for the two types of can are different.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• recycled cans do not need ore to be mined / quarried (1)\n• recycling uses much less energy than extraction by electrolysis (1)\n• less carbon dioxide released / fewer fossil fuels burned for recycled cans (1)\n• different transport needs, e.g. collecting used cans vs shipping ore (1)\n• recycled cans produce less waste / landfill (1)\nIgnore: cost` },
      { q: `Crude oil is the main source of fuels such as petrol and diesel, and of feedstock for making polymers. It formed over millions of years from the remains of ancient sea creatures.\n\nExplain why crude oil is described as a finite (non-renewable) resource.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• it takes millions of years to form / is formed very slowly (1)\n• it is being used up much faster than it is made / it will run out (1)` },
      { q: `Crude oil is separated in a fractionating column. Hot crude oil vapour enters near the bottom of the column. The column is hot at the bottom and cooler at the top.\n\nExplain why different fractions are collected at different heights in the column.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• fractions have different boiling points / molecules of different chain lengths have different boiling points (1)\n• each fraction condenses (and is collected) where the temperature falls below its boiling point (1)\nAllow: larger molecules condense lower down / smaller molecules rise higher before condensing` },
      { q: `The table shows the boiling points of four alkanes.\n\nAlkane | Formula | Boiling point (°C)\nmethane | CH₄ | −162\nethane | C₂H₆ | −89\npropane | C₃H₈ | −42\nbutane | C₄H₁₀ | −1\n\nExplain the trend in boiling point shown in the table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• boiling point increases as the number of carbon atoms / size of molecule increases (1)\n• because the forces between molecules (intermolecular forces) are stronger, so more energy is needed to separate the molecules (1)\nDo not allow: covalent bonds break / bonds within molecules are stronger (0 for second mark)` },
      { q: `Large alkane molecules from crude oil can be cracked to make smaller, more useful molecules.\n\nTetradecane, C₁₄H₃₀, is cracked to make octane and propene:\n\nC₁₄H₃₀ → C₈H₁₈ + 2 ……………\n\nComplete the equation and name the type of hydrocarbon that propene is.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• C₃H₆ (1)\n• (propene is an) alkene / unsaturated hydrocarbon (1)\nAllow: C3H6` },
      { q: `The table shows the supply and demand for two fractions from crude oil at a refinery (data written for this practice question).\n\nFraction | Supply (% of crude oil) | Demand (% of crude oil)\npetrol | 12 | 28\nheavy fuel oil | 30 | 14\n\nUse the data to explain why the refinery cracks some of the heavy fuel oil.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• supply of heavy fuel oil (30%) is greater than demand (14%) and demand for petrol (28%) is greater than supply (12%) (1)\n• cracking converts the larger (heavy fuel oil) molecules into smaller molecules such as petrol, to match supply with demand (1)\nData must be used for first mark.` },
      { q: `Water, H₂O, is a simple covalent molecule. An oxygen atom has 6 electrons in its outer shell. A hydrogen atom has 1 electron.\n\nDraw a dot and cross diagram to show the bonding in a water molecule. Show the outer shell electrons only.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• two shared pairs of electrons, each made of one dot and one cross, between O and each H (1)\n• two non-bonding pairs (four further electrons) on the oxygen atom, giving 8 outer electrons on O (1)\nIgnore: inner shells if drawn correctly\nIgnore: circles` },
      { q: `Methane, CH₄, is the main gas in natural gas. It boils at −162 °C.\n\nExplain why methane has a low boiling point. Use ideas about bonding and forces in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• methane is made of small / simple molecules (1)\n• the forces between molecules (intermolecular forces) are weak, so little energy is needed to separate them (1)\nDo not allow: covalent bonds are weak / covalent bonds break on boiling (0 for second mark)` },
      { q: `A teacher uses a ball and stick model to show a methane molecule. Black balls represent carbon atoms and white balls represent hydrogen atoms. The sticks represent covalent bonds.\n\nGive two limitations of using this ball and stick model to represent a methane molecule.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• it does not show the electrons / shared pairs of electrons (1)\n• atoms / balls are not the correct relative sizes (1)\n• bonds are not solid sticks / bonds are shown as too long or atoms too far apart (1)\n• atoms are not coloured (1)\n• it does not show the forces between molecules (1)\n• it is static, molecules / atoms actually move or vibrate (1)` },
      { q: `A student electrolyses sodium chloride solution in a small electrolysis cell with carbon electrodes. Two test tubes are filled with the solution and placed over the electrodes to collect the gases. A gas is collected at each electrode.\n\nDescribe how the student can test to show which gas is chlorine and which gas is hydrogen.\n\nGive the result of each test.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• chlorine: (place) damp (blue) litmus paper in the gas (1)\n• it turns red then bleaches / turns white (1)\n• hydrogen: lighted splint gives a (squeaky) pop (1)\nAllow: damp indicator paper / universal indicator paper is bleached\nDo not allow: glowing splint for hydrogen` },
      { q: `Aluminium is extracted by electrolysis of aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite. The electrodes are made of carbon.\n\nThe half equation at the anode is:\n2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻\n\nWrite the half equation for the reaction at the cathode, and explain why the carbon anodes must be replaced regularly.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al (1)\n• oxygen is produced at the (hot) anode (1)\n• oxygen reacts with the carbon to form carbon dioxide, so the anode burns away / wears away (1)\nAllow: multiples in the half equation, e.g. 4Al³⁺ + 12e⁻ → 4Al\nAllow: carbon monoxide for carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `Hydrogen chloride is made by reacting hydrogen with chlorine.\n\nH–H + Cl–Cl → H–Cl + H–Cl\n\nThe table shows some bond energies.\n\nBond | Bond energy (kJ/mol)\nH–H | 436\nCl–Cl | 242\nH–Cl | 431\n\nCalculate the overall energy change for the reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• energy to break bonds = 436 + 242 = 678 (kJ) (1)\n• energy released making bonds = 2 × 431 = 862 (kJ) (1)\n• energy change = 678 − 862 = −184 (kJ/mol) (1)\nCorrect answer of −184 with no working scores 3 marks.\nAllow: 184 kJ released / given out for 3 marks.\nAllow ECF from incorrect break or make values.\n+184 scores 2 marks.` },
      { q: `A student adds 1.0 g of zinc powder to 25 cm³ of copper sulfate solution in a polystyrene cup. The student stirs the mixture and records the highest temperature reached.\n\nTemperature at start = 21.0 °C\nHighest temperature = 33.5 °C\n\nCalculate the temperature change, state whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic, and suggest one way the student could make the result more accurate.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• temperature change = 33.5 − 21.0 = 12.5 (°C) (1)\n• exothermic, because the temperature increases / energy is given out to the surroundings (1)\n• any one improvement:\n– put a lid on the cup / insulate the cup (to reduce energy loss) (1)\n– use a thermometer with a higher resolution / a digital temperature probe (1)\n– measure the temperature at regular intervals and extrapolate back to find the maximum (1)\nIgnore: repeat the experiment (improves repeatability, not accuracy)` },
      { q: `Methane burns in oxygen. The reaction is exothermic.\n\nCH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O\n\nDraw a reaction profile for this reaction.\n\nLabel the reactants, the products, the activation energy and the overall energy change.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• products drawn at a lower energy level than reactants, with reactants and products labelled (1)\n• curve rises from reactants to a peak and falls to products, with activation energy shown as an arrow from the reactant level up to the peak (1)\n• overall energy change shown as an arrow from the reactant level down to the product level (1)\nAllow: arrow without arrowhead if clearly between correct levels\nAxes: energy (vertical) and progress of reaction (horizontal) need not be labelled for credit` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide, CO₂, is a simple covalent molecule. A carbon atom has 4 outer shell electrons and an oxygen atom has 6 outer shell electrons. The atoms in CO₂ are arranged O C O.\n\nDraw a dot and cross diagram for a molecule of carbon dioxide. Show the outer shell electrons only.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• two shared pairs (a double bond) between carbon and each oxygen atom, i.e. four electrons between C and each O (1)\n• each oxygen atom has two non-bonding pairs (1)\n• carbon has no non-bonding electrons and every atom has 8 outer electrons; dots and crosses used consistently to show the origin of electrons (1)\nIf single bonds are drawn between C and O, maximum 1 mark.` },
      { q: `The table shows some information about three fractions from crude oil.\n\nFraction | Number of carbon atoms | Boiling range (°C) | How easily it ignites | Viscosity\nrefinery gases | 1–4 | below 25 | very easily | very low\npetrol | 5–10 | 40–110 | easily | low\nbitumen | more than 35 | above 400 | very difficult | very high\n\nUse the information in the table to explain why petrol is used as a fuel for cars but bitumen is not.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• petrol ignites easily / is flammable but bitumen is very difficult to ignite (1)\n• petrol has low viscosity so flows easily (through fuel pipes / injectors), bitumen is very viscous (1)\n• petrol is a liquid at room temperature that vaporises easily / has a low boiling range, bitumen has a very high boiling range / is (almost) solid (1)\nIgnore: petrol is cheaper` },
      { q: `The table shows the energy needed to make 1 kg of aluminium (data written for this practice question).\n\nSource of aluminium | Energy needed (MJ per kg)\nextracted from ore by electrolysis | 200\nmade from recycled cans | 10\n\nA local council says: "Recycling aluminium cans is better for the environment than making new aluminium from ore."\n\nEvaluate the council's statement. Use the data and your own knowledge.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `For the statement, any two from:\n• recycling uses 190 MJ less per kg / only 5% of the energy / saves 95% of the energy (1)\n• less fuel burned so less carbon dioxide released (if electricity comes from fossil fuels) (1)\n• no mining / quarrying of bauxite, so less damage to landscape / habitats / less waste rock (1)\n• conserves a finite resource (ore) (1)\nAgainst, any one from:\n• energy / fuel and emissions from collecting, transporting and sorting cans (1)\n• cans must be cleaned / separated from other waste (1)\n• not all cans are returned, so some new aluminium is still needed (1)\nMaximum 3 marks; at least one point each way needed for 3 marks.` },
      { q: `Natural gas is mainly methane. When methane burns completely, it reacts with oxygen to make carbon dioxide and water.\n\nCH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O\n\nIn displayed formulae, methane has four C–H bonds, each oxygen molecule has one O=O bond, carbon dioxide has two C=O bonds and each water molecule has two O–H bonds.\n\nThe table shows some bond energies.\n\nBond | Bond energy (kJ/mol)\nC–H | 413\nO=O | 498\nC=O | 805\nO–H | 464\n\nCalculate the overall energy change for the reaction and explain what your answer shows about the reaction.\n\nEnergy needed to break bonds = …………… kJ\nEnergy released making bonds = …………… kJ\nEnergy change of reaction = …………… kJ/mol`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• bonds broken = (4 × 413) + (2 × 498) = 1652 + 996 = 2648 (kJ) (1)\n• bonds made = (2 × 805) + (4 × 464) = 1610 + 1856 = 3466 (kJ) (1)\n• energy change = 2648 − 3466 = −818 (kJ/mol) (1)\n• exothermic, because more energy is released making (new) bonds than is needed to break bonds (1)\nAllow ECF from incorrect break or make values.\nAllow: 818 kJ released for third mark.\nIgnore: sign in the final mark explanation if 'exothermic' and reason given.` },
      { q: `Copper is used for electrical wiring. Most copper is extracted from high-grade ores, but these are running out. A company is deciding how to obtain copper in the future.\n\nThe table compares three methods (data written for this practice question).\n\nMethod | Ore used | Time to obtain copper | Energy used (GJ per tonne of copper) | Other information\nsmelting (heating ore in a furnace, then purifying by electrolysis) | high-grade ore, 2% copper | days | 30 | produces sulfur dioxide; large open-pit mines\nbioleaching (bacteria make a solution of copper ions) | low-grade waste ore, 0.3% copper | months | 8 | acidic solution can leak into groundwater\nphytoextraction (plants absorb copper, then are burned) | contaminated soil, 0.1% copper | 1–2 years | 5 | needs large areas of land; cleans up the soil\n\nCompare the three methods and evaluate which method is the most sustainable way for the company to obtain copper.\n\nIn your answer you should:\n• use the data in the table\n• explain how copper metal is obtained from the copper compounds produced by bioleaching and phytoextraction\n• make a judgement.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nCompares all three methods using data AND explains how copper is obtained from the leachate / ash AND makes a reasoned judgement about sustainability.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nCompares at least two methods using some data AND EITHER describes how copper is obtained from solution OR makes a judgement with a reason.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nMakes simple statements about advantages or disadvantages of at least one method.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nUse of data\n• smelting is fastest (days) but uses most energy (30 GJ per tonne), about 4 times bioleaching and 6 times phytoextraction\n• smelting releases sulfur dioxide (acid rain) and more carbon dioxide from fuels; open-pit mines damage landscape\n• bioleaching and phytoextraction use low-grade or waste ores, conserving high-grade ore\n• bioleaching takes months; risk of acidic, metal-containing solution polluting groundwater\n• phytoextraction is slowest (1–2 years), lowest energy, needs large land area, but cleans contaminated soil\nObtaining copper\n• leachate / solution from ash contains copper ions / copper compounds\n• scrap iron added: iron is more reactive and displaces copper (iron + copper sulfate → iron sulfate + copper)\n• or copper is obtained by electrolysis of the solution, copper forms at the cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu\nJudgement\n• e.g. bioleaching / phytoextraction more sustainable because less energy and uses waste ores, but slower and smaller amounts, so smelting may still be needed to meet demand\n• any reasoned judgement that is supported by the data is credited` }
    ]
  },
  'C4': {
    name: 'Material Choices',
    questions: [
      { q: `Floor tiles are often made from a clay ceramic.\nWhen a heavy pan is dropped on a ceramic tile, the tile cracks into pieces. It does not bend or dent first.\n\nWhich word describes this property of the ceramic?\nTick (✓) one box.\nBrittle\nDuctile\nFlexible\nMalleable`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• Brittle (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one box ticked` },
      { q: `Carpet fibres can be made from an addition polymer.\nThe monomer used to make the polymer is propene, CH₂=CHCH₃.\n\nName the polymer made from propene.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• poly(propene) (1)\nALLOW polypropene / polypropylene\nDO NOT ALLOW polyethene` },
      { q: `Silver nanoparticles are added to some sports socks to kill bacteria.\n\nWhich diameter could be the diameter of a silver nanoparticle?\nTick (✓) one box.\n0.05 nm\n50 nm\n5000 nm\n5 mm`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• 50 nm (1)\nNanoparticles are 1–100 nm across.` },
      { q: `Buckminsterfullerene is one of the allotropes of carbon. Each molecule is a hollow sphere of carbon atoms.\n\nWhat is the molecular formula of buckminsterfullerene?\nTick (✓) one box.\nC₂\nC₂₀\nC₆₀\nC₆₀H₆₀`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• C₆₀ (1)` },
      { q: `A company carries out a life-cycle assessment (LCA) for aluminium drinks cans.\nThe first stage of the LCA looks at mining bauxite ore and extracting aluminium from it.\n\nWhat is the name of this stage of the life cycle?`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• obtaining / extracting the raw materials (1)\nALLOW sourcing raw materials\nIGNORE manufacture` },
      { q: `Pencil 'leads' are made from graphite mixed with clay.\nWhen a pencil is moved across paper, layers of graphite are left on the paper.\n\nWhy do the layers of graphite rub off onto the paper so easily?`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• forces between the layers are weak, so the layers slide off / over each other (1)\nALLOW there are no covalent bonds between the layers\nDO NOT ALLOW the covalent bonds are weak / bonds between atoms break` },
      { q: `Mia investigates the stiffness of strips of different plastics.\nShe clamps one end of each strip to the edge of a bench so that 20 cm sticks out. She hangs a 100 g mass on the free end and measures how far the end of the strip bends down.\n\nGive two variables that Mia must keep the same so that her test is fair.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• length of strip sticking out from the bench (1)\n• thickness of the strip (1)\n• width of the strip (1)\n• mass hung on the end / force applied (1)\n• position of the mass on the strip (1)\nIGNORE type of plastic (this is the independent variable)\nIGNORE amount / size of strip unqualified` },
      { q: `Kai has a block of an unknown metal. He finds its mass is 54.0 g and its volume is 20.0 cm³.\nThe table shows the densities of four metals.\nMetal | Density in g/cm³\nAluminium | 2.70\nTitanium | 4.51\nIron | 7.87\nCopper | 8.96\n\nCalculate the density of the block and use the table to identify the metal.\nUse the equation: density = mass ÷ volume`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• density = 54.0 ÷ 20.0 = 2.70 (g/cm³) (1)\n• aluminium (1)\nALLOW ECF for identification from an incorrect density` },
      { q: `A student wants to put four materials in order of hardness: a copper sheet, a steel nail, a glass slide and a piece of poly(propene).\n\nDescribe how the student could use a scratch test to put the materials in order of hardness.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• try to scratch each material with each of the other materials (1)\n• the material that scratches all the others / is not scratched by any of the others is the hardest (1)\nALLOW a harder material scratches a softer one\nALLOW using the same force each time for second marking point if hardness idea is given` },
      { q: `The spark plug in a petrol engine has a ceramic insulator around the central metal electrode. The temperature inside the engine can reach 900 °C.\n\nExplain why a ceramic is a good material for this insulator.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ceramics have a high melting point / do not soften at high temperatures (1)\n• ceramics do not conduct electricity, so current only passes across the electrode gap (1)\nALLOW ceramics are electrical insulators\nALLOW hard / not worn away for one mark if neither point above given` },
      { q: `Racing bicycle frames are often made from carbon fibre reinforced plastic. This is a composite material made of carbon fibres held in a polymer resin.\n\nExplain why the composite is a better material for the frame than the polymer resin alone.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• carbon fibres are strong (in tension) / stiff, so the frame is stronger / stiffer than resin alone (1)\n• resin binds the fibres together and keeps the low density / light weight, so the frame is strong but light (1)\nALLOW a composite combines the properties of both materials` },
      { q: `Bridges are often made from reinforced concrete. This is concrete with steel rods running through it.\nConcrete is strong in compression but weak in tension.\n\nExplain why steel rods are added to the concrete.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• steel is strong in tension (1)\n• so the composite is strong in both compression and tension / the bridge does not crack when it bends or is stretched (1)` },
      { q: `Poly(ethene) is made from ethene, C₂H₄.\n\nComplete the sentences about this reaction. Use words from the list.\nEach word can be used once, more than once or not at all.\n\naddition   condensation   double   monomers   polymers   single\n\nEthene molecules are the ………… that join to make poly(ethene).\nThe ………… bond between the carbon atoms opens so that the molecules can join together.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• monomers (1)\n• double (1)` },
      { q: `Water pipes can be made from poly(chloroethene), PVC.\nThe monomer chloroethene has the displayed formula: a C=C double bond; one carbon atom is bonded to two H atoms; the other carbon atom is bonded to one H atom and one Cl atom.\n\nDraw the displayed formula of the repeating unit of poly(chloroethene).`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• two carbon atoms joined by a single C–C bond with three H atoms and one Cl atom (1)\n• bonds extending from each carbon atom out through brackets, with n after the brackets (1)\nALLOW Cl on either carbon\nDO NOT ALLOW C=C in the repeating unit for first mark` },
      { q: `Polystyrene is used to make yoghurt pots. The repeating unit of polystyrene is –CH₂–CH(C₆H₅)–, where C₆H₅ is a ring of carbon atoms.\n\nDraw the displayed formula of the monomer used to make polystyrene.\nYou may show the C₆H₅ group as C₆H₅.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• C=C double bond between the two carbon atoms (1)\n• two H atoms on one carbon; one H atom and the C₆H₅ group on the other carbon (1)\nALLOW CH₂=CHC₆H₅ shown as displayed formula\nDO NOT ALLOW brackets or n` },
      { q: `Rubber in car tyres is vulcanised. During vulcanising, sulfur atoms form cross-links between the polymer chains.\n\nExplain why vulcanised rubber is harder and does not soften when the tyre gets hot.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cross-links are (strong) covalent bonds between the chains (1)\n• so the chains cannot slide past each other / move apart (when heated) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW cross-links are intermolecular forces` },
      { q: `The table shows the melting points of three samples of poly(ethene) with different average chain lengths.\n(data written for this practice question)\nSample | Average number of carbon atoms in a chain | Melting point in °C\nA | 2 000 | 105\nB | 20 000 | 125\nC | 200 000 | 137\n\nExplain the pattern in the melting points.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the longer the chains, the higher the melting point (1)\n• longer chains have stronger / more intermolecular forces between them, so more energy is needed to separate the chains (1)\nALLOW longer chains get more tangled\nDO NOT ALLOW covalent bonds break on melting` },
      { q: `Polyesters are condensation polymers. A polyester can be made by reacting a diol with a dicarboxylic acid.\n\nDescribe two ways in which condensation polymerisation is different from addition polymerisation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• a small molecule (e.g. water) is also made in condensation / addition polymerisation makes only the polymer (1)\n• condensation monomers have two functional groups / addition monomers have a C=C double bond (1)\n• condensation often uses two different monomers / addition can use one type of monomer (1)\nALLOW the reverse argument for each point` },
      { q: `Nylon is used to make ropes. Nylon is made by reacting a dicarboxylic acid with a diamine.\nEach dicarboxylic acid molecule has a –COOH group at both ends. Each diamine molecule has an –NH₂ group at both ends.\n\nExplain why each monomer must have a functional group at both ends to form a polymer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• each monomer can react / join at both ends (1)\n• so a long chain can keep growing in both directions (rather than stopping after one link) (1)\nALLOW with only one group, only two molecules could join` },
      { q: `Diamond is used on the tips of drills for cutting through rock.\n\nExplain why diamond is very hard. Use ideas about structure and bonding.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• each carbon atom is covalently bonded to four other carbon atoms in a giant (lattice) structure (1)\n• many strong covalent bonds must be broken to scratch / break it (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW intermolecular forces / ions / molecules` },
      { q: `Graphite is used to make the electrodes in some electrolysis cells.\n\nExplain why graphite conducts electricity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• each carbon atom forms (covalent bonds with) only three other carbon atoms (1)\n• so there are delocalised electrons that can move through the structure / along the layers (1)\nALLOW free electrons\nDO NOT ALLOW ions move` },
      { q: `Graphene is a single layer of graphite. It is one atom thick.\nScientists are developing flexible touch screens made from graphene.\n\nGive two properties of graphene that make it useful for touch screens and explain one of them.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• two from: conducts electricity / very strong / transparent / flexible / light (1)\n• explanation: delocalised electrons can move through the layer (conducts) OR strong covalent bonds between carbon atoms (strong) OR only one atom thick so lets light through (transparent) (1)` },
      { q: `Carbon nanotubes are cylinders of carbon atoms. They are a type of fullerene. Carbon nanotubes are added to the polymer used to make some tennis rackets.\n\nSuggest two reasons why adding carbon nanotubes improves the rackets.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• nanotubes are very strong / stiff (because of strong covalent bonds), so the racket is stronger / stiffer (1)\n• nanotubes have a low density / small mass, so the racket stays light (1)` },
      { q: `Copper is used for electrical wiring in homes.\n\nExplain how copper conducts electricity. Use ideas about metallic bonding.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• copper has a lattice of positive ions in a sea of delocalised electrons (1)\n• the delocalised electrons move through the metal / carry the charge when a voltage is applied (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW ions moving / free protons` },
      { q: `Pure gold is too soft for jewellery. Gold rings are made from an alloy of gold with copper and silver.\n\nExplain why the alloy is harder than pure gold.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the alloy contains atoms of different sizes (which distort the layers) (1)\n• so the layers cannot slide over each other as easily (1)\nALLOW in pure gold the layers of atoms slide easily for one mark if no other mark given` },
      { q: `Some medical dressings contain silver nanoparticles. The particles have a diameter of 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ m.\n1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m\n\nCalculate the diameter of the particles in nm and explain whether they are nanoparticles.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ ÷ 1 × 10⁻⁹ = 25 nm (1)\n• yes, because nanoparticles are between 1 and 100 nm (1)\nALLOW ECF on an incorrect conversion` },
      { q: `Some sun creams contain nanoparticles of titanium dioxide. Older sun creams contained larger particles of titanium dioxide and left a white layer on the skin.\n\nGive one benefit and one possible risk of using titanium dioxide nanoparticles in sun cream.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• benefit: transparent / does not leave a white layer / gives better protection from UV / covers skin more evenly (1)\n• risk: nanoparticles may pass through the skin / be breathed in / enter cells and cause harm / effects on health or the environment are not yet known (1)` },
      { q: `A supermarket compares two types of carrier bag.\n(data written for this practice question)\nBag | Energy to make one bag in MJ | Mass of CO₂ produced making one bag in g | What happens at end of life\nPaper | 1.7 | 300 | biodegrades\nPoly(ethene) | 1.4 | 200 | sent to landfill; does not biodegrade\n\nGive one advantage and one disadvantage of the poly(ethene) bag compared with the paper bag. Use the information in the table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• advantage: needs less energy to make / produces less CO₂ (1)\n• disadvantage: does not biodegrade / takes up landfill space for a long time (1)\nALLOW numerical comparisons, e.g. 0.3 MJ less energy, 100 g less CO₂` },
      { q: `Aluminium is extracted from aluminium oxide by electrolysis. This uses a lot of electricity.\nRecycling aluminium cans uses about 5% of the energy needed to extract the same mass of new aluminium.\n\nGive two other reasons why recycling aluminium cans is better for the environment than extracting new aluminium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• less bauxite needs to be mined / conserves the ore (1)\n• less damage to landscape / habitats from mining (1)\n• less waste sent to landfill (1)\n• less carbon dioxide released (from generating electricity) (1)\nIGNORE uses less energy (given in the question)` },
      { q: `Steel car bodies are made mainly from iron. If the paint is scratched, the steel starts to rust.\n\nExplain one method of preventing a steel car body from rusting.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• method: paint / coat with oil or grease / galvanise (coat with zinc) (1)\n• explanation: forms a barrier so that water and oxygen cannot reach the iron OR zinc is more reactive than iron so it reacts / corrodes instead of the iron (sacrificial protection) (1)` },
      { q: `Kai is given a rectangular block of metal. He measures the block: length 2.50 cm, width 2.00 cm, height 1.00 cm. The mass of the block is 44.6 g.\nThe table shows the densities of four metals.\nMetal | Density in g/cm³\nAluminium | 2.70\nTitanium | 4.51\nIron | 7.87\nCopper | 8.96\n\nCalculate the density of the block and identify the metal.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\nUse the equation: density = mass ÷ volume`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• volume = 2.50 × 2.00 × 1.00 = 5.00 cm³ (1)\n• density = 44.6 ÷ 5.00 = 8.92 g/cm³ (1)\n• copper (1)\nALLOW ECF for identification from an incorrect density` },
      { q: `Mia repeats her test on one plastic strip four times. She hangs a 100 g mass on the end of the strip and measures how far the end bends down.\n(data written for this practice question)\nTest | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nDistance bent in mm | 12.1 | 12.4 | 15.9 | 12.2\n\nIdentify the anomalous result and calculate the mean distance bent. Give your answer to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• anomalous result is 15.9 mm (test 3) (1)\n• (12.1 + 12.4 + 12.2) ÷ 3 (1)\n• = 12.2 mm (1)\nALLOW 12.23 for second marking point\nIf anomaly included: (12.1 + 12.4 + 15.9 + 12.2) ÷ 4 = 13.15 / 13.2 scores 1 mark (calculation of a mean) only` },
      { q: `Diamond and graphite are both made only of carbon atoms.\nDiamond is used for cutting tools. Graphite is used as a lubricant.\n\nExplain why graphite is soft and slippery but diamond is very hard. Use ideas about structure and bonding.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• in diamond each carbon atom forms four strong covalent bonds in a 3-D giant structure (1)\n• in graphite each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds in flat layers / hexagonal sheets (1)\n• the forces between the layers in graphite are weak so the layers slide over each other (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW covalent bonds between layers are weak` },
      { q: `Platinum is used as a catalyst in car exhausts. Scientists compare two cube-shaped particles of platinum.\nParticle X has sides of 20 nm. Particle Y has sides of 1000 nm.\nFor a cube: surface area = 6 × (side)²; volume = (side)³\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of each particle. Use your answers to state how many times larger the ratio is for particle X than for particle Y.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• X: 6 × 20² = 2400 nm²; 20³ = 8000 nm³; ratio = 0.3 (nm⁻¹) (1)\n• Y: 6 × 1000² = 6 000 000 nm²; 1000³ = 1 000 000 000 nm³; ratio = 0.006 (nm⁻¹) (1)\n• 0.3 ÷ 0.006 = 50 times larger (1)\nALLOW ratios written as 3:10 and 6:1000 (3:500)\nALLOW ECF for third mark from incorrect ratios` },
      { q: `Ethene, C₂H₄, is a gas at room temperature. Poly(ethene) is a solid at room temperature.\nBoth substances contain only covalent C–C (or C=C) and C–H bonds.\n\nExplain why poly(ethene) has a much higher melting point than ethene.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ethene is made of small molecules; poly(ethene) molecules are very long chains (1)\n• the intermolecular forces between poly(ethene) chains are much stronger than between ethene molecules (1)\n• so more energy is needed to separate the poly(ethene) chains / overcome the forces (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW covalent bonds break on melting for second or third mark` },
      { q: `Nanoparticles of cerium oxide, CeO₂, are added to some sun creams to absorb ultraviolet light.\nLena says: "Using nanoparticles in sun cream is completely safe."\n\nEvaluate Lena's statement. In your answer give arguments for and against the statement.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• for: protects skin from UV so reduces risk of sunburn / skin cancer (1)\n• against: nanoparticles are very small so may pass into the skin / body cells, or be washed into the environment, with effects that are not fully known (1)\n• conclusion: the statement is not justified because the long-term risks have not been fully tested / we cannot be sure it is completely safe (1)` },
      { q: `A shop compares two types of shopping bag.\n(data written for this practice question)\nBag | Energy to make one bag in MJ | Typical number of times used\nPaper | 1.7 | 2\nWoven poly(propene) | 6.0 | 50\n\nUse the data to calculate the energy used per use for each bag. Use your answers to decide which bag is the better choice for energy use.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• paper: 1.7 ÷ 2 = 0.85 MJ per use (1)\n• poly(propene): 6.0 ÷ 50 = 0.12 MJ per use (1)\n• the woven poly(propene) bag is better because it uses less energy per use (1)\nALLOW ECF for conclusion from incorrect values` },
      { q: `Eggs are often sold in boxes made from expanded polystyrene, a polymer made from crude oil. Some egg boxes are made from recycled paper pulp instead.\n\nExplain why the life-cycle assessments for the two types of egg box are different. Give three reasons.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• raw materials: polystyrene uses crude oil, a finite resource; paper pulp uses recycled paper / trees (renewable) (1)\n• manufacture: different amounts of energy / water / pollution in making each box (1)\n• use: different number of times each box can be reused (1)\n• disposal: paper pulp biodegrades / can be composted; polystyrene does not biodegrade / goes to landfill / releases toxic gases or CO₂ when burned (1)\n• transport: different masses so different fuel use (1)` },
      { q: `Polyester fibres are used in clothing. A polyester is made from two monomers.\nMonomer 1 is a dicarboxylic acid: HOOC–□–COOH\nMonomer 2 is a diol: HO–■–OH\n(□ and ■ represent the rest of each molecule.)\nWhen the monomers react, a –COOH group joins to an –OH group.\n\nDraw the repeating unit of the polyester and name the other product of the reaction. Explain why this is called condensation polymerisation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• ester link shown: –□–C(=O)–O–■–O– with C=O drawn (1)\n• correct repeating unit containing one □ and one ■ in brackets with bonds through brackets and n (1)\n• other product is water / H₂O (1)\n• condensation because a small molecule (water) is lost each time two monomers join (1)\nALLOW –OC–□–COO–■–O– for first two marks` },
      { q: `A company designs a new bicycle frame. It considers three materials. The table shows some data about the materials.\n(data written for this practice question)\nMaterial | Type of material | Density in g/cm³ | Strength in tension (relative) | Stiffness (relative) | Resistance to corrosion | Energy to make 1 kg in MJ | Easy to recycle?\nSteel | alloy | 7.9 | 10 | 10 | rusts unless painted | 30 | yes\nAluminium alloy | alloy | 2.7 | 6 | 4 | good | 160 | yes\nCarbon fibre reinforced polymer | composite | 1.6 | 12 | 8 | very good | 300 | no\n\nThe company wants a frame that is light and strong, and has a low impact on the environment.\nWhich material should the company choose?\nUse the information in the table and your knowledge of materials.\nIn your answer you should:\n• compare the properties of the three materials\n• discuss the environmental impact of each material over its life cycle\n• explain your choice.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Compares the properties of all three materials using data AND discusses life-cycle / environmental impact of each AND makes a justified choice.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Compares properties of at least two materials using data AND discusses some environmental impact, OR makes a choice supported by some reasoning.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Makes simple statements about the properties or environmental impact of the materials, OR makes a choice with limited reasoning.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nProperties\n• CFRP has the lowest density (1.6) so the lightest frame; steel is about 5 times denser (7.9)\n• CFRP is the strongest in tension (12); aluminium alloy weakest (6)\n• steel is the stiffest (10); aluminium alloy least stiff (4)\n• steel rusts so needs painting / maintenance; aluminium and CFRP resist corrosion so last longer\nLife cycle\n• steel needs least energy to make (30 MJ/kg); CFRP most (300 MJ/kg) so more CO₂ / fossil fuel used\n• but a lighter frame uses less material: e.g. same volume of CFRP has about one-fifth the mass of steel\n• steel and aluminium can be recycled at end of life, saving ore and energy; CFRP is hard to recycle so goes to landfill\n• longer life (no corrosion) reduces impact per year of use\nChoice\n• any choice is acceptable if justified, e.g. CFRP for light and strong, accepting higher energy and no recycling; or aluminium alloy as a compromise (light, corrosion-resistant, recyclable); or steel for lowest manufacturing energy and recyclability but heavy` }
    ]
  },
  'C5': {
    name: 'Chemical Analysis',
    questions: [
      { q: `Some everyday products are formulations.\n\nWhich of these products is a formulation?\n\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\n\ncopper wire        distilled water        gloss paint        sodium chloride`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• gloss paint (1)\nIf more than one answer is ringed, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Mia does a flame test on a white solid from a bottle with a missing label. The flame turns lilac.\n\nName the metal ion in the solid.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Potassium (ion) / K⁺ (1)\nDo not allow: potassium atom / sodium` },
      { q: `Ben uses chromatography to investigate the dyes in a felt-tip pen. The dyes do not dissolve in water.\n\nWhich solvent could Ben use instead of water?\n\nTick (✓) one box.\n\n☐ dilute hydrochloric acid\n☐ ethanol\n☐ limewater\n☐ salt solution`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• ethanol only ticked (1)\nIf more than one box is ticked, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Kai tests a solution of fertiliser for sulfate ions. He adds a few drops of dilute hydrochloric acid and then a few drops of another solution. A white precipitate forms.\n\nName the solution Kai adds after the dilute hydrochloric acid.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Barium chloride (solution) (1)\nAllow: barium nitrate\nAllow: BaCl₂\nIgnore: concentration` },
      { q: `Nina reads the volume of acid in a burette at the end of a titration. The bottom of the meniscus is exactly halfway between the 23.4 cm³ and 23.5 cm³ marks.\n\nWrite down the burette reading Nina should record.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• 23.45 (cm³) (1)\nDo not allow: 23.4 / 23.5 / 23.450` },
      { q: `Alex carries out a titration. Alex adds acid from a burette to 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution in a conical flask. An indicator is used to show the end-point.\n\nName a suitable indicator for this titration.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Phenolphthalein / methyl orange (1)\nDo not allow: universal indicator / litmus` },
      { q: `A carton of apple juice has the label "100% pure apple juice – nothing added".\n\nA chemist says the apple juice is not pure.\n\nExplain why the chemist and the label use the word "pure" differently.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• On the label, pure means natural / nothing has been added to it (1)\n• To a chemist, a pure substance contains only one substance / one element or one compound, but apple juice is a mixture (of water, sugars, acids, etc.) (1)` },
      { q: `Pure aspirin has a melting point of 136 °C. A student makes some aspirin in the laboratory. The student measures its melting point using a melting point apparatus.\n\nThe student's aspirin starts to melt at 126 °C and has completely melted at 132 °C.\n\nIs the student's aspirin pure? Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Not pure / contains impurities (1)\n• It melts over a range of temperatures and/or below 136 °C; a pure substance melts at a single, fixed temperature (1)\nAllow: impurities lower the melting point and make it melt over a range` },
      { q: `Jamal uses paper chromatography to analyse a green food colouring.\n\nThe solvent front moves 7.5 cm from the start line. A yellow spot moves 4.2 cm from the start line.\n\nCalculate the Rf value of the yellow spot.\n\nUse the equation: Rf = distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4.2 ÷ 7.5 (1)\n• = 0.56 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks.\nDo not allow: answers with units / 1.79 (inverted)` },
      { q: `A food scientist analyses an orange drink using paper chromatography (data written for this practice question).\n\nThe orange drink gives two spots with Rf values 0.31 and 0.74.\n\nThe table shows the Rf values of four permitted food dyes in the same solvent.\n\nDye | Rf value\nE102 | 0.74\nE110 | 0.52\nE124 | 0.31\nE129 | 0.43\n\nIdentify the dyes in the orange drink. Explain how you know the orange drink is a mixture.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• E102 and E124 (1)\n• The drink gives more than one spot (two spots), so it contains more than one substance (1)` },
      { q: `Sam has a mixture of sand and salt water. He wants to obtain a sample of the salt water with no sand in it.\n\nDescribe how Sam can do this, and explain why the method works.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Filter the mixture / pour it through filter paper in a funnel (1)\n• Sand is insoluble so it stays on the filter paper (as the residue) and the salt solution passes through (as the filtrate) (1)` },
      { q: `Priya makes copper sulfate crystals from copper sulfate solution. She heats the solution in an evaporating basin until crystals start to form at the edge. She then stops heating and leaves the solution to cool.\n\nExplain why Priya stops heating instead of evaporating all the water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Copper sulfate crystals contain water / are hydrated, so heating to dryness would turn them into (white) powder / decompose them (1)\n• Slow cooling allows larger / well-formed crystals to grow (1)\n• Any soluble impurities stay in the remaining solution (1)\n• Heating to dryness may cause spitting (of hot solution) (1)` },
      { q: `A group of students on a sailing trip wants to make drinking water from sea water. They set up a simple distillation apparatus: a flask of sea water heated by a burner, connected to a water-cooled condenser that leads to a collecting beaker.\n\nExplain how the apparatus produces pure water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Water boils / evaporates (from the flask) and the salt is left behind (because it has a much higher boiling point) (1)\n• The water vapour cools and condenses in the condenser and the liquid (pure) water is collected (1)` },
      { q: `A mixture of ethanol and water is separated by fractional distillation.\n\nEthanol boils at 78 °C. Water boils at 100 °C.\n\nWhich liquid is collected first? Explain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Ethanol (1)\n• It has the lower boiling point, so it evaporates first / reaches the top of the fractionating column first (1)` },
      { q: `A technician needs to separate three different mixtures.\n\nDraw lines to connect each mixture with the best method to separate it.\n\nMixture:\n• salt from salt solution\n• pure water from ink\n• different coloured dyes from ink\n\nMethod:\n• chromatography\n• crystallisation\n• filtration\n• simple distillation`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `• salt from salt solution → crystallisation\n• pure water from ink → simple distillation\n• different coloured dyes from ink → chromatography\nAll three correct = 2 marks; one or two correct = 1 mark.\nMore than one line from a mixture: that line scores 0.` },
      { q: `Leo does flame tests on some metal compounds. Before each test he dips a nichrome wire loop in concentrated hydrochloric acid and holds it in a roaring Bunsen burner flame until the flame shows no colour.\n\nExplain why Leo cleans the wire in this way before each test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• To remove any traces of the previous sample / other metal compounds (1)\n• Which would also colour the flame and give a false / misleading result (1)` },
      { q: `A solid contains a mixture of sodium chloride and potassium chloride. A student does a flame test on the solid. The student sees only a yellow flame.\n\nExplain why a flame test is not suitable for showing that potassium ions are present in the solid, and suggest a better method.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The yellow colour of sodium hides / masks the lilac colour of potassium (1)\n• Use flame emission spectroscopy (which shows the separate lines of both metals) (1)\nAllow: view the flame through blue (cobalt) glass for the first mark only if linked to hiding of the colour` },
      { q: `Jo adds a few drops of dilute sodium hydroxide solution to three solutions of metal compounds. A precipitate forms each time.\n\nComplete the table to show the colour of each precipitate.\n\nSolution | Colour of precipitate\ncopper(II) sulfate | ………\niron(II) sulfate | ………\niron(III) chloride | ………`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• copper(II): (light) blue\n• iron(II): green\n• iron(III): orange-brown / red-brown / rust coloured\nAll three correct = 2 marks; two correct = 1 mark.\nDo not allow: brown for iron(II)` },
      { q: `A water company tests a sample of water from a well. Adding a few drops of sodium hydroxide solution to the water gives a white precipitate. A flame test on a dried sample of the solid from the water gives an orange-red flame.\n\nName the metal ion in the water. Explain how both results support your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Calcium ions / Ca²⁺ (1)\n• White precipitate with sodium hydroxide shows a metal ion such as calcium, and the orange-red (brick-red) flame identifies it as calcium (1)` },
      { q: `Ali tests a solution for halide ions. He adds dilute nitric acid to the solution and then adds silver nitrate solution.\n\nExplain why Ali adds dilute nitric acid before adding silver nitrate solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• To remove carbonate ions (1)\n• Which would also form a (white) precipitate with silver nitrate / give a false positive result (1)` },
      { q: `A student tests a solution of a salt. She adds dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution. A cream precipitate forms.\n\nName the negative ion in the salt and give the formula of the precipitate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Bromide (ion) / Br⁻ (1)\n• AgBr (1)\nDo not allow: bromine / Br₂ for the first mark` },
      { q: `Geologists find a white rock that they think contains a carbonate.\n\nDescribe a test the geologists could do to show that the rock contains carbonate ions. Give the result if carbonate ions are present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Add dilute (hydrochloric) acid to the rock and bubble the gas through limewater (1)\n• Fizzing / bubbles and the limewater turns milky / cloudy (carbon dioxide made) (1)` },
      { q: `Kai tests a solution of sodium sulfate for sulfate ions. He adds dilute hydrochloric acid and then barium chloride solution. A white precipitate forms.\n\nComplete the word equation for the reaction.\n\nbarium chloride + sodium sulfate → ………… + …………`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• barium sulfate (1)\n• sodium chloride (1)\nEither order.` },
      { q: `A forensic scientist uses flame emission spectroscopy to analyse soil from a suspect's shoe. The table shows the wavelengths of the main lines in the spectra of some metals (values rounded to the nearest nm).\n\nMetal | Wavelengths of lines (nm)\ncalcium | 423\nlithium | 610, 671\npotassium | 766, 770\nsodium | 589\n\nThe soil sample gives lines at 423 nm, 766 nm and 770 nm.\n\nIdentify the metals in the soil sample. Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• Calcium and potassium (1)\n• The sample has lines at exactly the same wavelengths as calcium (423 nm) and potassium (766 and 770 nm), and no lines for lithium or sodium (1)` },
      { q: `A hospital laboratory uses flame emission spectroscopy to measure the concentration of sodium ions in diluted blood samples. The intensity of the sodium line is measured for some standard solutions (data written for this practice question).\n\nConcentration of sodium ions (mg/dm³) | Intensity (arbitrary units)\n2.0 | 20\n4.0 | 40\n6.0 | 60\n\nA diluted blood sample gives an intensity of 50.\n\nEstimate the concentration of sodium ions in the diluted sample. Explain how you used the data.`, marks: 2, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `• 5.0 mg/dm³ (1)\n• Intensity is (directly) proportional to concentration / intensity = 10 × concentration, so 50 lies halfway between 40 and 60 (1)` },
      { q: `A technician makes a solution to use in an experiment. She dissolves 12.5 g of copper sulfate in water and makes the volume up to 250 cm³.\n\nCalculate the concentration of the solution in g/dm³.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 250 cm³ = 0.250 dm³ (1)\n• 12.5 ÷ 0.250 = 50 (g/dm³) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks.\nAllow 1 mark for 0.05 (g/cm³ not converted)` },
      { q: `Hydrogen chloride gas, HCl, dissolves in water to make hydrochloric acid. A sample of hydrochloric acid contains 7.3 g of hydrogen chloride.\n\nRelative atomic masses: H = 1.0, Cl = 35.5\n\nCalculate the amount, in moles, of hydrogen chloride in the sample.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mr HCl = 36.5 (1)\n• 7.3 ÷ 36.5 = 0.20 (mol) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Amir does a titration five times. His results are shown in the table.\n\nTitration | Rough | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nVolume of acid (cm³) | 25.30 | 24.70 | 24.75 | 25.10 | 24.65\n\nAmir uses only concordant results (within 0.10 cm³ of each other) to calculate the mean.\n\nCalculate the mean titre.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Uses 24.70, 24.75 and 24.65 only (ignores rough and 25.10) (1)\n• (24.70 + 24.75 + 24.65) ÷ 3 = 24.70 (cm³) (1)\nAllow 1 mark for 24.80 (all four non-rough results) or 24.90 (all five)` },
      { q: `Ella reacts marble chips with dilute hydrochloric acid and collects the carbon dioxide in a gas syringe. She repeats the experiment four times with the same mass of marble chips (data written for this practice question).\n\nVolumes of gas collected (cm³): 47, 53, 50, 50\n\nCalculate the mean volume and estimate the uncertainty in the mean.\n\nGive your answer in the form: mean ± uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mean = 200 ÷ 4 = 50 (cm³) (1)\n• Uncertainty = half the range = (53 − 47) ÷ 2 = ±3, so 50 ± 3 cm³ (1)` },
      { q: `Methane burns in oxygen:\n\nCH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nAll gas volumes are measured at the same temperature and pressure.\n\nCalculate the volume of oxygen needed to burn 50 cm³ of methane completely, and the volume of carbon dioxide made.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Oxygen: 100 cm³ (1)\n• Carbon dioxide: 50 cm³ (1)\n(Equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of molecules, so volumes are in the ratio 1 : 2 : 1.)` },
      { q: `Ben uses paper chromatography to investigate a black ink. He puts a spot of the ink on a pencil line and stands the paper in a beaker of water. After 10 minutes the ink has separated into a blue spot near the top and a red spot near the bottom.\n\nExplain why the blue and red dyes separate. Use ideas about the mobile phase and the stationary phase in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The mobile phase is the solvent / water which moves up the paper; the stationary phase is the paper (1)\n• Dyes move between the phases / each dye is attracted to the paper and dissolves in the solvent to different extents (1)\n• The blue dye spends more time in the mobile phase / is more soluble in the solvent / is less attracted to the paper, so it travels further (1)` },
      { q: `Zara sets up a paper chromatography experiment to find out which dyes are in a sweet coating.\n\nHere is her method:\n• Draw a start line on the paper in felt-tip pen.\n• Put a spot of the dye solution on the line.\n• Stand the paper in a beaker with the solvent level above the start line.\n• Leave the paper in the beaker until the solvent reaches the top of the paper.\n\nZara's method will not give good results. Suggest three improvements to her method. Give a reason for each.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three from (improvement with reason):\n• Draw the start line in pencil, because pencil does not dissolve / felt-tip ink would also separate and give extra spots (1)\n• Keep the solvent level below the start line, so the dye does not dissolve into the solvent in the beaker (1)\n• Remove the paper before the solvent reaches the top and mark the solvent front, so Rf values can be calculated (1)\n• Put a lid on the beaker, so the solvent does not evaporate / the air stays saturated with solvent vapour (1)\n• Run reference dyes on the same paper, so the dyes can be identified by comparison (1)` },
      { q: `Alex collects a sample of a mineral from an old mine. Alex dissolves the mineral in water and does some tests. The table shows the results.\n\nTest | Result\nflame test | green flame\nadd a few drops of sodium hydroxide solution | light blue precipitate\nadd dilute nitric acid then silver nitrate solution | white precipitate\n\nIdentify the compound in the mineral. Explain how the results show this.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• Copper chloride / copper(II) chloride / CuCl₂ (1)\n• Green flame and blue precipitate (with NaOH) show copper(II) ions / Cu²⁺ (1)\n• White precipitate with (acidified) silver nitrate shows chloride ions / Cl⁻ (1)` },
      { q: `A student makes a solution of sodium hydroxide, NaOH, for a titration. She dissolves 2.0 g of sodium hydroxide in water and makes the solution up to 250 cm³.\n\nRelative atomic masses: Na = 23.0, O = 16.0, H = 1.0\n\nCalculate the concentration of the solution in mol/dm³.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mr NaOH = 40 and moles = 2.0 ÷ 40 = 0.050 (mol) (1)\n• Volume = 250 ÷ 1000 = 0.250 dm³ (1)\n• Concentration = 0.050 ÷ 0.250 = 0.20 (mol/dm³) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks.\nAllow ECF from an incorrect number of moles.` },
      { q: `A company makes a drain cleaner that contains sodium hydroxide. A chemist checks the concentration of hydrochloric acid used to test the drain cleaner.\n\n25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ sodium hydroxide solution is exactly neutralised by 22.50 cm³ of the hydrochloric acid.\n\nNaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O\n\nCalculate the concentration of the hydrochloric acid in mol/dm³. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Moles NaOH = 0.100 × 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.00250 (mol) (1)\n• Moles HCl = 0.00250 (mol) (1 : 1 ratio) (1)\n• Concentration HCl = 0.00250 ÷ 0.02250 = 0.111 (mol/dm³) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks.\nAllow 2 marks for 0.1111 or 0.11 (not to 3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to make hydrogen gas.\n\nMg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂\n\nA student reacts 0.60 g of magnesium with excess acid.\n\nRelative atomic mass: Mg = 24\nOne mole of any gas occupies 24 dm³ at room temperature and pressure.\n\nCalculate the volume of hydrogen made at room temperature and pressure. Give your answer in cm³.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Moles Mg = 0.60 ÷ 24 = 0.025 (mol) (1)\n• Moles H₂ = 0.025 (mol) (1 : 1 ratio) (1)\n• Volume = 0.025 × 24 = 0.60 dm³ = 600 (cm³) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks.\nAllow 2 marks for 0.60 dm³ (not converted to cm³)` },
      { q: `Limestone is mainly calcium carbonate. When calcium carbonate is heated it decomposes:\n\nCaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂\n\nA student heats 12.5 g of pure calcium carbonate until it has completely decomposed.\n\nRelative formula masses: CaCO₃ = 100, CaO = 56\n\nCalculate the mass of calcium oxide made.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Moles CaCO₃ = 12.5 ÷ 100 = 0.125 (mol) (1)\n• Moles CaO = 0.125 (mol) (1 : 1 ratio) (1)\n• Mass CaO = 0.125 × 56 = 7.0 (g) (1)\nAlternative: 100 g gives 56 g, so 12.5 g gives 56 × 12.5 ÷ 100 = 7.0 g\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks.` },
      { q: `Nina does a titration three times. Her titres are 22.40 cm³, 22.50 cm³ and 22.30 cm³.\n\nShe then does a fourth titration and gets a titre of 22.65 cm³.\n\nUse the first three results to calculate the mean titre and its uncertainty. Explain whether the fourth result is within the uncertainty of the mean.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mean = (22.40 + 22.50 + 22.30) ÷ 3 = 22.40 (cm³) (1)\n• Uncertainty = half the range = (22.50 − 22.30) ÷ 2 = ±0.10 (cm³) (1)\n• 22.65 is outside the range 22.30–22.50 cm³, so it is not within the uncertainty / it is anomalous (1)` },
      { q: `Car batteries contain sulfuric acid. A technician checks the concentration of some battery acid by titration with sodium hydroxide solution.\n\n25.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol/dm³ sodium hydroxide solution is exactly neutralised by 20.00 cm³ of the diluted sulfuric acid.\n\n2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O\n\nCalculate the concentration of the diluted sulfuric acid in mol/dm³.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Moles NaOH = 0.200 × 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.00500 (mol) (1)\n• Moles H₂SO₄ = 0.00500 ÷ 2 = 0.00250 (mol) (2 : 1 ratio) (1)\n• Volume of acid = 20.00 ÷ 1000 = 0.02000 dm³ (1)\n• Concentration = 0.00250 ÷ 0.02000 = 0.125 (mol/dm³) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 4 marks.\nAllow 3 marks for 0.250 (mole ratio not used).` },
      { q: `A company sells a kettle descaler that contains an acid. Sam wants to find the concentration of the acid in the descaler by titration with sodium hydroxide solution of known concentration.\n\nSam has this apparatus: a burette, a 25.0 cm³ pipette with filler, a conical flask, a white tile, a funnel and phenolphthalein indicator.\n\nDescribe how Sam should carry out the titration to obtain accurate and repeatable results.\n\nIn your answer you should:\n• describe the method\n• explain how Sam can make his results accurate and repeatable.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Describes a full, logically ordered method AND explains several ways of making the results accurate and repeatable.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes most of the method AND gives some ways of making the results accurate or repeatable.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is in the most part relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Describes some steps of the method OR gives a way of improving accuracy.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• Use the pipette (and filler) to put 25.0 cm³ of the (diluted) descaler into the conical flask\n• Add a few drops of phenolphthalein indicator\n• Fill the burette with sodium hydroxide solution using a funnel; remove the funnel; record the starting reading\n• Add the alkali to the acid, swirling the flask\n• Stop at the end-point, when the indicator turns (permanently) pink; record the final reading\n• Titre = final reading − starting reading\nAccuracy and repeatability\n• Rinse the burette with the sodium hydroxide solution and the pipette with the descaler first\n• Read the burette at eye level, at the bottom of the meniscus\n• Make sure the burette jet is filled / no air bubble below the tap\n• Do a rough titration first, then add alkali drop by drop near the end-point\n• Use the white tile to see the colour change clearly\n• Repeat until concordant results (within 0.10 cm³) and calculate the mean of the concordant titres` }
    ]
  },
  'C6': {
    name: 'Making Useful Chemicals',
    questions: [
      { q: `Mia tests some solutions with universal indicator. Lemon juice, vinegar and dilute hydrochloric acid all turn the indicator red or orange.\n\nName the ion that is present in all acidic solutions.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Hydrogen ion / H⁺ (1)\nAllow: H⁺(aq)\nDo not allow: hydrogen / H / H₂ / hydroxide ion` },
      { q: `Sodium hydroxide is a strong alkali. A student makes a dilute solution of sodium hydroxide in water.\n\nWhich pH value is most likely for this solution?\n\nTick (✓) one box.\n\n☐ pH 1\n☐ pH 5\n☐ pH 7\n☐ pH 13`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• pH 13 only ticked (1)\nIf more than one box is ticked, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Hydrogen peroxide decomposes slowly to make water and oxygen. Jo adds 0.5 g of black manganese dioxide powder to some hydrogen peroxide. The mixture fizzes quickly. At the end Jo filters, dries and weighs the black powder. It still has a mass of 0.5 g.\n\nWhat is the job of the manganese dioxide in this reaction?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• (It is a) catalyst / it speeds up the reaction without being used up (1)\nIgnore: it reacts with hydrogen peroxide` },
      { q: `Ammonia is made in industry by the Haber process. Nitrogen and hydrogen are passed over a catalyst at about 450 °C and 200 atmospheres pressure.\n\nName the catalyst used in the Haber process.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Iron (1)\nAllow: Fe\nIgnore: references to promoters / iron oxide` },
      { q: `Ali investigates the rate of reaction between magnesium ribbon and dilute hydrochloric acid. Hydrogen gas is made. Ali wants to measure the volume of hydrogen made every 10 seconds.\n\nName a piece of apparatus Ali could use to measure the volume of gas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Gas syringe (1)\nAllow: (upturned) measuring cylinder / burette filled with water over a trough\nDo not allow: beaker / conical flask / balance` },
      { q: `When blue hydrated copper sulfate crystals are heated they turn white. When water is added to the white powder it turns blue again and gets hot.\n\nhydrated copper sulfate ⇌ anhydrous copper sulfate + water\n\nWhat does the symbol ⇌ tell you about this reaction?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• The reaction is reversible / it can go in both directions (1)\nAllow: products can react to form the reactants again\nIgnore: equilibrium (on its own)` },
      { q: `Sam adds green copper carbonate powder to dilute sulfuric acid in a beaker. The mixture fizzes and the solution turns blue.\n\nComplete the word equation for the reaction.\n\ncopper carbonate + sulfuric acid → ………………………… + water + …………………………`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• copper sulfate (1)\n• carbon dioxide (1)\nEither order.\nDo not allow: copper sulfide / carbon monoxide / hydrogen` },
      { q: `Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to make magnesium chloride and hydrogen.\n\nThe formula of magnesium chloride is MgCl₂.\n\nWrite a balanced symbol equation for this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• Correct formulae: Mg + HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (1)\n• Correctly balanced: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (1)\nAllow: state symbols (ignore if incorrect)\nAllow: correct multiples\nDo not allow: H instead of H₂ for the balancing mark` },
      { q: `Priya reacts marble chips with dilute hydrochloric acid and collects the carbon dioxide in a gas syringe. Her results are shown (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime (s) | 0 | 20 | 40 | 60 | 80\nVolume of carbon dioxide (cm³) | 0 | 21 | 35 | 45 | 51\n\nCalculate the mean rate of reaction over the first 60 seconds.\n\nGive the unit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 (1)\n• cm³/s / cm³ s⁻¹ (1)\nThe unit mark is independent.\nAllow: 45 cm³/min for both marks` },
      { q: `Kai collects the hydrogen given off when zinc reacts with dilute sulfuric acid. He plots a graph of volume of hydrogen (cm³) against time (s) and draws a smooth curve.\n\nKai draws a tangent to the curve at 20 s. The tangent is a straight line that passes through the points (0 s, 16 cm³) and (40 s, 64 cm³) (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the rate of reaction at 20 s.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Gradient = (64 − 16) ÷ (40 − 0) (1)\n• = 1.2 (cm³/s) (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working.` },
      { q: `A soil scientist measures the pH of two samples of rainwater. Sample A has pH 3. Sample B has pH 5.\n\nHow many times greater is the hydrogen ion concentration in sample A than in sample B?\n\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• 100 (times) (1)\n• pH changes by 2 / decreasing pH by 1 increases the H⁺ concentration by a factor of 10, so 10 × 10 (1)\nDo not allow: 2 times / 20 times` },
      { q: `Leo makes copper sulfate solution. He warms some dilute sulfuric acid and adds black copper oxide powder, one spatula at a time, stirring after each addition. He keeps adding copper oxide until some black solid is left in the beaker.\n\nExplain why Leo adds copper oxide until some solid is left over.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (So the copper oxide is in excess and) all of the acid is used up / reacts / is neutralised (1)\n• So the solution contains only copper sulfate (and water) / no acid is left in the salt / the extra solid can be removed by filtering (1)` },
      { q: `Leo filters his copper sulfate solution and pours the filtrate into an evaporating basin. He heats the solution until about half of the water has evaporated, then leaves the basin in a warm place for a few days.\n\nExplain why Leo does not heat the solution until all of the water has gone.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Large / well-shaped crystals form when the solution cools / evaporates slowly (1)\n• Heating to dryness gives a powder / small crystals OR may decompose the salt / removes the water of crystallisation / may cause spitting (1)` },
      { q: `Ruby does a titration to find the volume of dilute hydrochloric acid needed to neutralise 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution. Her results are shown (data written for this practice question).\n\nTitration | Rough | 1 | 2 | 3\nVolume of acid (cm³) | 22.50 | 22.15 | 22.25 | 22.20\n\nCalculate the mean titre. Use only Ruby's accurate results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (22.15 + 22.25 + 22.20) ÷ 3 (1)\n• = 22.20 (cm³) (1)\nAllow: 22.2 (cm³)\nIf the rough titre is included: (22.50 + 22.15 + 22.25 + 22.20) ÷ 4 = 22.275 / 22.28, award 1 mark` },
      { q: `A technician dissolves 5.85 g of sodium chloride in water and makes the solution up to exactly 250 cm³ in a volumetric flask.\n\nCalculate the concentration of the solution in g/dm³.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 250 cm³ = 0.250 dm³ OR 5.85 ÷ 0.250 (1)\n• = 23.4 (g/dm³) (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working.\nAllow 1 mark for 0.0234 (no unit conversion)` },
      { q: `Hydrogen is made in industry by reacting methane with steam.\n\nCH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂\n\nRelative formula masses: CH₄ = 16, H₂O = 18, CO = 28, H₂ = 2\n\nCalculate the atom economy for making hydrogen by this reaction.\n\nUse the equation: atom economy = (relative formula mass of desired product ÷ sum of relative formula masses of all reactants) × 100`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mass of desired product = 3 × 2 = 6 AND total mass of reactants = 16 + 18 = 34 (1)\n• Atom economy = (6 ÷ 34) × 100 = 17.6 (%) (1)\nAllow: 18% / 17.65%\nAllow 1 mark for (2 ÷ 34) × 100 = 5.9%` },
      { q: `Some washing powders contain enzymes that break down food stains. Ella tests how well a biological washing powder removes an egg stain at different temperatures. The stain is removed fastest at 40 °C. At 70 °C the stain is hardly removed at all.\n\nExplain why the washing powder removes the stain much more slowly at 70 °C than at 40 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The enzyme is denatured (at high temperature) (1)\n• The shape of the active site changes / the substrate no longer fits the enzyme, so it no longer catalyses the reaction (1)\nDo not allow: the enzyme is killed (for the first mark)` },
      { q: `Hydrochloric acid and ethanoic acid solutions are made with the same concentration, 0.1 mol/dm³.\n\nThe hydrochloric acid has pH 1. The ethanoic acid has pH 3.\n\nExplain why the two acids have different pH values.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Hydrochloric acid is a strong acid and is fully / completely ionised (in water) (1)\n• Ethanoic acid is a weak acid and is only partly ionised, so it has a lower concentration of H⁺ ions (1)\nAllow: dissociated for ionised\nDo not allow: ethanoic acid is more dilute` },
      { q: `Two students are talking about a bottle of acid labelled "dilute ethanoic acid".\n\nNina says: "Dilute and weak mean the same thing."\n\nExplain why Nina is wrong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Dilute means there is a small amount of acid in a given volume of solution / low concentration (1)\n• Weak means the acid is only partly ionised in water (1)` },
      { q: `Ammonium chloride is heated in a sealed tube. Some ammonium chloride breaks down to ammonia and hydrogen chloride, and some ammonia and hydrogen chloride react to form ammonium chloride again.\n\nNH₄Cl ⇌ NH₃ + HCl\n\nAfter a while the amounts of each substance in the tube stop changing. The tube has reached dynamic equilibrium.\n\nDescribe what is happening to the reactions in the tube at dynamic equilibrium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• The forward and backward reactions are both still happening / have not stopped (1)\n• at the same rate (so the amounts / concentrations stay constant) (1)\nDo not allow: the amounts of reactants and products are equal` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide, NO₂, is a brown gas. Dinitrogen tetroxide, N₂O₄, is a colourless gas. They are in equilibrium in a sealed gas syringe.\n\n2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)\nbrown        colourless\n\nThe plunger of the syringe is pushed in so that the pressure increases. The temperature stays the same.\n\nPredict how the colour of the gas mixture changes once equilibrium is re-established. Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `• (Becomes) paler / less brown (1)\n• The equilibrium moves to the right / towards N₂O₄ because there are fewer molecules (of gas) on the right (1 mole of gas compared with 2) (1)\nAllow: position of equilibrium shifts to reduce the pressure\nIgnore: the mixture initially goes darker when compressed` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide, NO₂, is a brown gas. Dinitrogen tetroxide, N₂O₄, is a colourless gas. They are in equilibrium in a sealed flask.\n\n2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)\nbrown        colourless\n\nThe forward reaction is exothermic. The flask at room temperature is placed in a beaker of ice and water.\n\nPredict and explain what you would see.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `• The gas becomes paler / less brown / more colourless (1)\n• Lowering the temperature moves the equilibrium in the exothermic direction / to the right / towards more N₂O₄ (1)` },
      { q: `In the Haber process only about 15% of the nitrogen and hydrogen is converted into ammonia each time the gases pass through the reactor. The ammonia is removed by cooling the mixture so that the ammonia condenses.\n\nExplain why the unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen are recycled back into the reactor.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• So that (eventually) most / about 98% of the reactants are converted to ammonia / the overall yield is high (1)\n• Reactants are not wasted, which reduces costs / saves raw materials / saves energy making more hydrogen (1)` },
      { q: `The decomposition of hydrogen peroxide is an exothermic reaction.\n\n2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂\n\nSketch a reaction profile for this reaction. Label the reactants, the products and the activation energy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `• Curve with a hump, with products (2H₂O + O₂) at a lower energy level than reactants (2H₂O₂), both labelled (1)\n• Activation energy shown by an arrow from the reactant level up to the top of the hump and labelled (1)\nDo not allow: activation energy arrow from the products level` },
      { q: `Ben investigates how temperature affects the rate of reaction between sodium thiosulfate solution and dilute hydrochloric acid. A yellow precipitate of sulfur forms. Ben puts the flask on a piece of paper with a black cross drawn on it and times how long it takes for the cross to disappear.\n\nGive two variables Ben must keep the same so that his test is fair.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Volume of sodium thiosulfate solution (1)\n• Concentration of sodium thiosulfate solution (1)\n• Volume of hydrochloric acid (1)\n• Concentration of hydrochloric acid (1)\n• Same cross / same paper / same size flask / same person judging (1)\nDo not allow: temperature / time` },
      { q: `Ben repeats his sodium thiosulfate experiment. His results at 30 °C are 41 s, 35 s and 47 s. Ben's teacher says the times are not very repeatable because it is hard to judge exactly when the cross disappears.\n\nSuggest how Ben could improve his method to get more repeatable results, and explain why this is an improvement.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• Use a light sensor / light meter / colorimeter (and data logger) to detect when the light passing through drops to a set value (1)\n• This removes the need for human judgement of when the cross disappears / the end point is the same each time (1)` },
      { q: `Grace investigates the reaction between marble chips and dilute hydrochloric acid. She puts the flask on a top-pan balance and puts a loose plug of cotton wool in the neck of the flask. She records the mass every 30 seconds.\n\nExplain why the mass of the flask and its contents decreases during the reaction, and why Grace uses the cotton wool plug.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Carbon dioxide gas is made and escapes (into the air) (1)\n• Cotton wool lets gas out but stops acid spray / liquid escaping (so only loss of gas is measured) (1)` },
      { q: `A company is choosing a new process to make a chemical. The engineers want the process to be as sustainable as possible.\n\nWhich two features would make the process more sustainable?\n\nTick (✓) two boxes.\n\n☐ It uses a catalyst that can be reused.\n☐ It runs at 900 °C.\n☐ It has a low atom economy.\n☐ It uses raw materials from plants that can be regrown.\n☐ It makes a toxic by-product.`, marks: 2, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• It uses a catalyst that can be reused (1)\n• It uses raw materials from plants that can be regrown (1)\nIf more than two boxes are ticked, deduct 1 mark for each extra tick.` },
      { q: `Zara makes some zinc sulfate crystals. She calculates that she should be able to make 8.0 g of crystals. She actually makes 6.0 g.\n\nCalculate the percentage yield.\n\nUse the equation: percentage yield = (actual yield ÷ theoretical yield) × 100`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (6.0 ÷ 8.0) × 100 (1)\n• = 75 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working.` },
      { q: `Much of the ammonia made by the Haber process is used to make fertilisers. Ammonia is a base. It reacts with nitric acid to make the salt ammonium nitrate, which farmers add to soil.\n\nThe formula of ammonium nitrate is NH₄NO₃.\n\nGive the name of the type of reaction between ammonia and nitric acid, and the element in ammonium nitrate that plants need for growth.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Neutralisation (1)\n• Nitrogen (1)\nIgnore: acid–base reaction for neutralisation\nDo not allow: nitrate / ammonia for the second mark` },
      { q: `Alex reacts marble chips with hydrochloric acid of two different concentrations, 0.5 mol/dm³ and 1.0 mol/dm³. All other variables are kept the same. The reaction with 1.0 mol/dm³ acid makes gas faster.\n\nExplain, using ideas about particles, why the more concentrated acid reacts faster.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (In the more concentrated acid) there are more acid particles in the same volume / particles are closer together (1)\n• So collisions (between acid particles and the marble) are more frequent / there are more collisions per second (1)\n• So there are more successful collisions per second (1)\nDo not allow: particles move faster / have more energy` },
      { q: `Tom reacts 2 g of magnesium ribbon with excess dilute hydrochloric acid. He then repeats the experiment with 2 g of magnesium powder. The magnesium powder finishes reacting much sooner than the ribbon.\n\nExplain why the powder reacts faster than the ribbon, and why the total volume of hydrogen made is the same in both experiments.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The powder has a larger surface area (to volume ratio) / more magnesium particles are exposed to the acid (1)\n• So there are more frequent collisions (between acid particles and magnesium) (1)\n• The same mass / amount of magnesium is used (and the acid is in excess), so the same amount of hydrogen is made (1)` },
      { q: `Leo makes copper sulfate from copper oxide and sulfuric acid.\n\nCuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O\n\nRelative formula masses: CuO = 79.5, CuSO₄ = 159.5\n\nLeo starts with 4.00 g of copper oxide. He makes 6.42 g of dry copper sulfate.\n\nCalculate the theoretical yield of copper sulfate and the percentage yield.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Theoretical yield = 4.00 × 159.5 ÷ 79.5 (1)\n• = 8.03 g (1)\n• Percentage yield = (6.42 ÷ 8.03) × 100 = 80.0 (%) (1)\nAllow: moles method: 4.00 ÷ 79.5 = 0.0503 mol, × 159.5 = 8.03 g\nAllow ECF from an incorrect theoretical yield for the third mark\nAllow: 80%` },
      { q: `A student reacts 0.60 g of magnesium with excess dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\nMg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂\n\nRelative atomic masses: Mg = 24, Cl = 35.5\n\nCalculate the maximum mass of magnesium chloride that could be made.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Mᵣ of MgCl₂ = 24 + (2 × 35.5) = 95 (1)\n• Moles of Mg = 0.60 ÷ 24 = 0.025 (mol) (1)\n• Mass of MgCl₂ = 0.025 × 95 = 2.38 (g) (1)\nAllow: 2.375 for 2 marks only if not given to 3 sig figs\nAllow ECF from an incorrect Mᵣ` },
      { q: `The Haber process makes ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen.\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nThe forward reaction is exothermic. A lower temperature would give a higher equilibrium yield of ammonia, but the process is run at about 450 °C.\n\nExplain why a temperature of about 450 °C is chosen.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• At a lower temperature the equilibrium yield would be higher because the forward reaction is exothermic (1)\n• But the rate of reaction would be too slow (at low temperature) (1)\n• 450 °C is a compromise between a reasonable yield and a fast enough rate (so ammonia is made quickly enough / economically) (1)\nAllow: the iron catalyst does not work well at low temperatures for the second mark` },
      { q: `Copper sulfate can be made by two different reactions.\n\nReaction 1: CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O\nReaction 2: CuCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O + CO₂\n\nRelative formula masses: CuO = 79.5, CuCO₃ = 123.5, H₂SO₄ = 98, CuSO₄ = 159.5\n\nThe atom economy of reaction 1 is 89.9%.\n\nCalculate the atom economy of reaction 2 and explain which reaction is better in terms of atom economy.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Total mass of reactants = 123.5 + 98 = 221.5 (1)\n• Atom economy = (159.5 ÷ 221.5) × 100 = 72.0 (%) (1)\n• Reaction 1 is better because it has a higher atom economy / less waste is made (reaction 2 also makes carbon dioxide as a waste product) (1)\nAllow: 72%\nAllow ECF for the third mark` },
      { q: `Ben measures the time for the cross to disappear in the reaction between sodium thiosulfate and hydrochloric acid at different temperatures (data written for this practice question).\n\nTemperature (°C) | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50\nTime (s) | 96 | 48 | 24 | 12\n\nDescribe the pattern in the results and use it to predict the time at 60 °C.\n\nExplain why the rate changes in this way, using ideas about particles.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• As the temperature increases (by 10 °C) the time halves / the rate doubles (1)\n• Predicted time at 60 °C = 6 (s) (1)\n• Particles have more energy / move faster, so more collisions have energy greater than the activation energy / more collisions are successful (per second) (1)` },
      { q: `Ali puts 25 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid in a beaker and adds dilute sodium hydroxide solution, 1 cm³ at a time. He measures the pH with a pH meter after each addition. The pH starts at 1, rises suddenly to about 7 after 25 cm³ has been added, and ends at about 13.\n\nExplain the changes in pH. Include the ionic equation for neutralisation in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• At the start there are (many) H⁺ ions / the solution is acidic, so the pH is low (1)\n• H⁺ ions react with OH⁻ ions to form water: H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O (1)\n• After neutralisation (about 25 cm³) there are excess OH⁻ ions / the solution is alkaline, so the pH is high (1)\nAllow: H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)` },
      { q: `Ruby uses a titration to find the concentration of some dilute sulfuric acid. She puts 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution of concentration 0.100 mol/dm³ in a conical flask with a few drops of indicator. Her mean titre of sulfuric acid is 22.20 cm³.\n\n2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O\n\nCalculate the concentration of the sulfuric acid in mol/dm³.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Moles of NaOH = 0.100 × 25.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.00250 (mol) (1)\n• Moles of H₂SO₄ = 0.00250 ÷ 2 = 0.00125 (mol) (1)\n• Concentration = 0.00125 ÷ (22.20 ÷ 1000) (1)\n• = 0.0563 (mol/dm³) (1)\nAllow ECF at each stage\nAllow 3 marks for 0.113 (mol/dm³) (1 : 1 ratio used)` },
      { q: `Ibuprofen is a painkiller. It has been made by two different industrial routes. The table shows some information about each route (approximate published values).\n\nFeature | Older route | Newer route\nNumber of steps | 6 | 3\nAtom economy | about 40% | about 77%\nCatalysts | used up, end as waste salts | recovered and reused\nMain by-products | a mixture of waste salts that must be disposed of | ethanoic acid, which can be collected and sold\n\nA chemical company says: "The newer route is a greener way to make ibuprofen."\n\nEvaluate this statement. Use the information in the table and your knowledge of green chemistry.\n\nIn your answer you should:\n• compare the two routes\n• explain how each feature affects sustainability and cost.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nCompares the two routes using several features from the table AND explains how these affect sustainability and cost, reaching a supported judgement.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nCompares the two routes using at least two features AND gives some explanation of how they affect sustainability or cost.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nIdentifies at least one difference between the routes OR makes a simple statement about green chemistry.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nComparison and explanation\n• Newer route has fewer steps, so less energy used / less equipment / less material lost at each step / higher overall yield likely\n• Newer route has higher atom economy (77% vs 40%), so more of the atoms in the reactants end up in the product / less waste\n• Less waste means lower disposal costs and less environmental harm\n• Reusable catalysts in the newer route mean fewer raw materials are used and less waste is made\n• The by-product of the newer route (ethanoic acid) is useful and can be sold, effectively raising the atom economy and reducing cost\n• Older route makes waste salts that must be treated / disposed of safely\nJudgement and limitations\n• Overall the statement is supported by the data\n• Other factors not given would also matter: energy needed / temperature and pressure of each step, the percentage yield, the hazards of reactants and solvents, whether raw materials are renewable, life cycle of the product` }
    ]
  }
};

if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) module.exports = CHEMISTRY_OCR_B_GCSE_PRACTICE;
